COPY public.shared_cards (id, shared_id, ord, front, back) FROM stdin;
4780df32-ab60-42e9-b892-873aae95c538	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	1	Which HLA is associated with DM in the lecture?	HLA-DQ8.
ed89ea72-69a6-4856-a1df-59e100e77ce5	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	2	What is the indication for skin transplantation?	Burns.
db6153ed-94d8-425f-9764-f4ace0ae3581	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	3	Which HLA is associated with ankylosing spondylitis?	HLA-B27.
8a898b72-d6d7-42c4-9672-0e672b770764	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	4	What are the indications for liver transplantation?	Cirrhosis, primary cancer and biliary atresia.
5a2d0203-e3c3-4e18-b633-9e022cb3b914	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	5	When does chronic rejection occur?	Months or years after transplantation.
b99830b2-7117-4a22-a019-cc28bae30aac	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	6	What is the transplant reaction in allogeneic grafts?	Rejection and GVH.
d6a0b33b-5404-4056-b83e-2e5592f50d11	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	7	What does transplantation involve?	Transfer of living cells, tissues or organs from one body site to another or from one individual to another.
3db974c8-a815-4794-beb8-952e451c792e	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	8	Which cells express MHC Class I?	All nucleated cells.
375a4210-48b4-4c51-b244-f305c00af4ac	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	9	What is the transplant reaction in xenogeneic grafts?	Rejection and GVH.
963f8615-a303-4bc6-955a-a00807b142ab	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	10	What are the mechanisms protecting the fetus from rejection?	Placental barrier, filtration of anti-MHC antibodies, weak or absent MHC expression on trophoblast, immunosuppressive progesterone and placental FasL expression.
13ab62fc-f37f-4d98-af8c-efbfc12ba9a4	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	11	What other immunosuppressive approach is listed?	Anti-T-cell antibodies.
9ec31c7b-424f-435a-b39e-4d83360dbf08	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	12	Which solid organ transplants are commonly affected by chronic rejection?	Heart, kidney, lung and liver.
5cdc39d8-ae5b-4ba5-b7ef-92f0c0bea1e8	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	13	How does Azathioprine act?	Blocks purine metabolism.
ac5cf16c-f8ab-47fa-974c-f07cb2d1f7cc	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	14	Which HLA loci belong to MHC Class II?	HLA-D, DR, DQ and DP.
e40def3c-1059-4097-9e64-25d6a6e264ad	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	15	What are the consequences of microthrombi in hyperacute rejection?	Ischemia and necrosis of the graft.
09c8b736-7b52-4da6-9c0b-a3a6ddc15bf2	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	16	How is cross-matching performed?	Mixing a small amount of recipient serum with a small amount of potential donor white blood cells.
f4fad1ce-f6d7-4cd3-aafc-09b737d0cf2c	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	17	What may trigger spontaneous abortion?	Maternal immune responses against the fetus.
a75a9237-e82e-4115-915d-e8038a4b2a92	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	18	What characterizes chronic GVHD?	Atrophy and fibrosis of target organs, including the skin, GI tract, liver and lungs.
b9ab5b70-d9db-4832-94e9-2e6743616511	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	19	What does MHC encode?	Polymorphic cell-surface glycoproteins involved in antigen recognition and presentation.
b0952495-ec5c-482f-9f72-7ebcc1696bb3	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	20	What does strong proliferation in MLR indicate?	High incompatibility.
d9a7f65e-9771-4f39-a603-272c07d9a07f	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	21	What is cross-matching in transplantation?	Testing recipient serum for antibodies against donor antigens.
ce299461-435b-4c0c-a95a-96613bdbf8c7	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	22	What happens to effector T cells during acute rejection?	They migrate from lymphoid tissue to tissues, including the graft.
50681919-33eb-4f9c-a11e-9ae837298e04	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	23	Which domains form the antigen-binding cleft of Class II MHC?	α1 and β1.
ef2a67b3-c1a9-4cf8-8d5b-4cc8c0ac21ee	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	24	What is autografting?	Transfer of self-tissue from one body site to another in the same individual.
ae401d1d-f249-417d-995a-6f1db8b9bd22	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	25	What are examples of autografting?	CABG, skin grafts, bone marrow transplantation and hair transplantation.
d645e1ae-a3a8-416c-bc71-bd5a2690b25e	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	26	What are the main transplantation antigens?	Major histocompatibility complex (MHC) antigens and minor histocompatibility antigens (MiHA).
3be2c0f2-2003-4c7b-8ef8-44c8962b2e8a	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	27	What characterizes acute GVHD?	Epithelial cell death in the skin, GI tract and liver.
3e6cacf6-6a7f-4650-a6c1-333455ed77ba	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	28	What is HLA tissue typing used for?	Identifying HLA molecules and assessing donor-recipient compatibility before transplantation.
067ca501-d252-4266-b449-8e65c3ddfa11	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	29	What are the four types of graft?	Autologous, isogenic (syngeneic), allogeneic and xenogeneic grafts.
32a00ff0-d496-4510-a6e2-bf56988f9914	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	30	What is graft-versus-host (GVH) disease?	An immune response in which immunologically active cells in the graft attack the host.
26a2ec86-2273-4f4b-a947-2a5c46c346e5	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	31	What causes acute rejection?	Classical cytotoxic mechanisms.
5bbd1eba-9931-4bc2-8a62-e04da6aee603	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	32	When was the first successful renal transplantation performed?	1954.
7dd45d98-1aaa-4bce-a210-6c19202d4148	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	33	What is OKT3?	A monoclonal antibody against the CD3 receptor on T cells.
0f596816-f71a-40e9-a1e7-a3e880ba2187	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	34	What is the Micro-Cytotoxicity Assay used for?	Immunologic assessment in donor-recipient workup.
efa6587e-b0ca-417b-8a57-5e6aa45328a4	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	35	What are corticosteroids used for in transplantation?	Non-specific immunosuppression by modulating gene expression and blocking inflammatory cytokine production.
14f5383e-f661-497b-aa9b-ed0b25a6b3fa	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	36	When does acute rejection commonly occur?	2–4 weeks after transplantation.
5d29f551-2068-4ba6-997b-b46f4b0a3b7e	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	37	What are the indications for lung or heart-lung transplantation?	Pulmonary hypertension and cystic fibrosis.
bba606c3-d2ce-4be4-9b34-d2e9761c5a6a	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	38	What is the mouse MHC called?	H-2.
a4288147-8c08-44d1-a0df-c62beffca52c	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	39	What are the indications for bone marrow transplantation?	Immune deficiency and leukemia.
2ba45049-d836-440d-893d-a548dbacf66c	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	40	Which cells and antibodies mediate acute rejection?	T cells and antibodies.
c6c57629-ea9a-49f9-95cd-f9dc79681326	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	41	Why can rejection still occur despite MHC matching?	Minor histocompatibility antigens remain.
926fbc6a-b06f-451b-a9a8-ffad3fc0512a	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	42	Which Class I MHC domain is highly conserved?	α3.
69316d3b-8f93-4315-b03a-88e377f8adb3	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	43	What mediates hyperacute rejection?	Antibodies against HLA and ABO blood group antigens.
0e986b10-c5be-40cf-8351-2c4930c4cfa8	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	44	What is the effect of MHC (HLA) matching on rejection?	It reduces rejection but does not eliminate it.
f5069514-23de-4f9f-be99-e40044eae09c	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	45	How many domains does the Class I MHC α chain have?	Three: α1, α2 and α3.
73fdca58-6d9a-42b2-bd9b-627c0a15919f	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	46	Into how many regions is human MHC divided?	Three regions encoding Class I, Class II and Class III molecules.
1a5a9f1c-4267-4bf0-b74b-af321a869066	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	47	What is the indication for kidney transplantation?	End-stage chronic renal failure (CRF).
57f69bfc-4d3b-4139-8bdb-6523d80f8092	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	48	What does MLR help identify?	Antigenic differences between donor and recipient.
0601fdff-af64-4052-96f2-9ebb621ddc9d	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	49	What is an autologous graft?	Transfer of tissue from one site to another within the same individual.
2595c02f-4a31-4772-a9f5-8ef300dddb72	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	50	What is the function of MHC Class II?	Interaction between immunocompetent cells.
bd59a38e-0fdc-4c84-804f-20c0176d5ea3	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	51	How can acute rejection be prevented?	Immunosuppressive drugs, ALG and monoclonal antibodies.
44bc9e32-ddc5-41dd-8cd1-6121b0374021	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	52	What are symptoms of GVH disease?	Skin rash, weight loss and diarrhea.
f07e6d2a-6f52-483e-a023-353a540ec4d1	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	53	Which immunosuppressive drugs are listed for preventing acute rejection?	Azathioprine, prednisolone, cyclosporine, CellCept, Prograf and Imuran.
abc6aa8f-e3f0-4e10-9b58-3f95fe3e2fd3	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	54	What are the components of the Class I MHC molecule?	An α chain and β2-microglobulin.
9b0134fc-5e62-42d3-bceb-ff6059955b8e	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	55	How does chronic rejection progress?	Very slowly.
51b2ce6a-aa66-4145-8e75-586b2331b53c	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	56	What is required for Class I MHC surface expression?	Association of the α chain with β2-microglobulin.
e3616d25-640d-4634-be13-471294d0982a	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	57	What characterizes chronic rejection?	Fibrosis and vascular abnormalities with prolonged loss of graft function.
8cc149e7-37e6-4f0a-ae14-2518c3952ebf	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	58	What must be compatible in donor and recipient blood grouping?	ABO blood group.
57d3a2fe-ef45-40d2-8935-80daea17a253	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	59	What is the molecular weight of the Class I MHC α chain?	45 kDa.
765e089d-3016-43cb-bd0c-9f3ba69c9a43	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	60	Why may recipient serum contain antibodies against donor HLA?	Previous blood transfusions, transplantation or multiple pregnancies.
c4799b12-f4b3-406e-bfdd-b4832e3d8e72	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	61	What is the function of MHC molecules?	Intercellular recognition and antigen presentation to T-lymphocytes.
ea9a0ea0-e625-474a-b3b8-c15f1fa9717f	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	62	What are the components of the Class II MHC molecule?	α and β chains.
35e9165f-c456-4734-997e-e06eb3d45b91	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	63	Where is the human MHC located?	On the short arm of chromosome 6.
2d5c4dea-a30c-44d5-a205-1caed8080a3e	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	64	What was the significance of OKT3?	It was the first monoclonal antibody approved for clinical use in humans.
e2d27ca8-2409-46cd-9018-69df39d4fce6	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	65	Which cells express MHC Class II?	Antigen-presenting cells, including macrophages, B cells and dendritic cells.
afc10962-2300-4ac7-a733-77d8aeba7db0	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	66	What are the consequences of chronic rejection?	Obliteration of blood vessels and interstitial fibrosis.
c8768ccb-c5f9-480c-aad0-57c3a90d374f	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	67	Which tissues are injured during acute rejection?	Vascular and parenchymal tissues.
c2ac9cdc-543e-49e9-9c1a-99b7ae1416a6	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	68	In which conditions may bone marrow transplantation be used after high-dose chemotherapy and radiation?	Lymphoma, leukemia, multiple myeloma and aplastic anemia.
1e86ef25-4d8f-4608-affe-d5442f2410fd	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	69	What are common causes of HLA sensitization?	Blood transfusions, pregnancies and previous transplants.
4f11ed0f-7b9a-473c-9864-074fabda5e61	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	70	Which T cells can destroy graft cells during acute rejection?	Both CD4 and CD8 T cells.
fa1cb92e-97ae-4384-839b-9c2eb78a56dc	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	71	What is an allogeneic (homologous) graft?	Graft between genetically different members of the same species.
39bc6200-30ac-420b-a2ee-62a2ca87fc68	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	72	What is an isogenic (syngeneic) graft?	Graft between genetically identical individuals.
6cecb730-2383-4caa-9749-74bc92861728	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	73	What is the role of MHC in self/non-self determination?	It plays a major role in self/non-self recognition.
f90d5a0a-71ef-4ad1-a4b0-1624c81a959f	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	74	What are the three types of host-versus-graft rejection?	Hyperacute, acute and chronic rejection.
801283aa-d8e4-4587-a17b-e48ae59b36f3	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	75	How is the Mixed Lymphocyte Reaction performed?	Donor lymphocytes are cultured together with recipient lymphocytes.
3e988708-9932-4b08-8cf7-2819c248edde	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	76	How can chronic rejection be minimized?	Maximizing histocompatibility between donor and recipient.
e656ccd0-0392-4738-9e00-f4f2d5ecc8e3	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	77	Can graft rejection occur despite identical MHC genes in donor and recipient?	Yes, amino acid differences in minor proteins can cause slow graft rejection.
686f949e-a3c3-4cea-b340-4e3da19ae51d	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	78	Which T-cell type recognizes endogenous antigen presented by Class I MHC?	CD8 cytotoxic T cells.
c725ee4b-bdb1-4fdd-b1e4-25ddf5824556	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	79	Which cells mediate chronic rejection?	Macrophages and T cells.
82fc1a36-dc81-45df-8871-6e2a730c0648	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	80	Which Class II MHC domains bind CD4?	α2 and β2.
e22619c8-b5ec-4222-8daa-02136f4d9ea7	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	81	When does acute rejection occur?	Days or weeks after transplantation.
42733108-a741-40ee-aee1-8e11341219c5	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	82	What does MHC Class II present?	Processed antigen fragments to CD4 T cells.
61fc2735-6399-4b30-b318-93656861cd7c	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	83	What are the three subgroups of the MHC gene family?	Class I, Class II and Class III.
2b6265dd-d6b0-4015-bde4-5624a952265b	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	84	What is the immunological effect of progesterone?	It is immunosuppressive.
0ec7162f-30a2-4eb3-8986-ef73f29f4915	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	85	Where are MHC proteins located?	On the surface of white blood cells and other tissues.
bc5dc453-5010-45cf-b240-44a5282d580a	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	86	What are the main features of acute rejection?	T-cell, macrophage and antibody-mediated injury, endothelial damage and inflammation.
f7306c45-4ba7-43a8-8aba-4a89af31846a	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	87	What is ALG?	Anti-lymphocyte globulin capable of destroying human T cells.
e36b5bad-91bc-4024-ad38-f1f945a71efa	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	88	How do cytotoxic drugs act as immunosuppressants?	They kill dividing cells by inhibiting DNA replication.
56911830-0ddd-43e9-924b-ea433341bda4	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	89	What is the trophoblast?	The outermost layer of fetal tissue in direct contact with maternal blood.
e5336e09-4b6c-47ca-a46a-4f720441621e	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	90	When does hyperacute rejection begin after anastomosis?	Within minutes or hours.
40aefaf8-66c1-435c-a939-ebf3cba2785d	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	91	What mechanisms are involved in chronic rejection?	Cell-mediated responses and deposition of antibodies or antigen-antibody complexes.
c1e2a11a-43a1-42c5-8af1-3a8c08721809	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	92	What are the indications for corneal transplantation?	Keratoconus, corneal degeneration, corneal scarring or perforation.
ddecc45b-8474-4445-b148-30a0a5808aeb	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	93	Which HLA is associated with SLE according to the lecture?	HLA-DR4.
d8889fad-f197-46bf-8587-7ccb32bd0e29	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	94	What is the Mixed Lymphocyte Reaction (MLR)?	A functional assay measuring lymphocyte proliferation in response to lymphocytes from another individual.
6563ee1d-afed-4005-a8ba-48486530da85	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	95	What are the established lung transplantation procedures?	Single lung and heart-lung transplantation.
7bbc4f66-a5b1-4676-8202-bad615a89d50	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	96	Why is immunosuppression needed after transplantation?	Because MHC matching alone cannot prevent rejection.
0db94fd2-ac14-403c-831d-aa465798496a	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	97	What is cross-matching?	A test to detect recipient serum antibodies directed against the potential donor's HLA antigens.
9f04b7db-189b-49fc-b62d-48c1e14c7455	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	98	When was the first successful human heart-lung transplant performed?	1981, in the USA.
071c7302-73e3-49be-b46f-3fde78ca94fb	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	99	What is activated after antibodies bind donor endothelium in hyperacute rejection?	Complement and the clotting pathway.
bb8b7db6-af82-4747-9035-eb2dee0bf3f6	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	100	Which domains form the peptide-binding cleft of Class I MHC?	α1 and α2.
c232b646-0a63-40bc-8ba5-d9d0726849e1	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	101	What is the transplant reaction in isogenic grafts?	No rejection and no GVH.
5314d251-600a-4a1e-b584-86440b3edd6a	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	102	What is the transplant reaction in autologous grafts?	No rejection and no GVH.
1371fb21-a6e5-4dff-8ee7-7edc575d5147	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	103	Which MHC molecules present antigen during acute rejection?	Class II HLA molecules.
2b26c596-2cf7-4b6a-99e0-0dc980c7729c	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	104	What is allografting?	Transfer of organs or tissues from one human to another.
6a3c4fef-e685-4328-8f66-bff0aecb158f	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	105	Why is MHC matching not possible for most grafts?	Extensive polymorphism and too few donors.
126d5e9e-2145-4045-b19e-f1efe1c7c0b3	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	106	Where is MHC Class I mainly distributed?	On almost every nucleated cell.
a66d9a13-130f-443d-a59d-2f83e8188a61	bce02944-8abc-461f-a46d-6173707bc423	107	Which corticosteroids are listed in the lecture?	Prednisone, hydrocortisone and prednisolone.
