98875c2f-3ecd-41bc-96e2-aeee2ed3aae1	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	598	What is the basic problem in conductive deafness?	Disturbed conduction of sound vibrations to the basilar membrane.
004ce182-c6e1-47ba-96d3-9559d287e46c	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	599	What does the olfactory tract divide into?	Three olfactory striae.
91825e94-3cdc-4a2f-b578-938c5b5b8885	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	600	How is the taste stimulus removed?	Saliva washes the substance away.
aa919456-e771-400d-bbd1-6154874ed5ea	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	601	Why does unilateral primary auditory cortex lesion cause only impaired hearing?	Both ears are bilaterally represented.
c2c817d4-07cb-48dc-8b09-65059e6211d5	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	602	What is the basic problem in perceptive deafness?	Disturbed perception of sound waves and their transmission to the cortex.
6e408a16-eec1-4f94-be5b-35f29af68f62	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	603	Which nerve carries taste from the posterior one-third of the tongue?	Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX).
8e4bebf2-eb05-41fe-9433-44f452710b5e	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	604	Where do low-frequency traveling waves reach maximum height?	Near the apex of the cochlea.
dd108cea-bf63-4ad5-b1aa-9abc961bbbc5	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	605	Which substances produce sweet taste?	Sugars, alcohols, aldehydes, and ketones.
d5adfe12-0c44-4879-9201-abfcdcc0665f	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	606	How many olfactory cells are present in each nostril?	Approximately 10 million.
574b7421-a419-4c80-8e83-5f9b8e0736d8	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	607	What does the Rinne test compare?	Bone conduction and air conduction in the same ear.
839c0507-74d4-4188-898b-b9332edb9fb2	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	608	What can an audiometer test?	Air and bone conduction.
e0e8d5c7-11d3-4ad3-82c4-b1f6cb61a1b3	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	609	What is the traveling wave theory of hearing?	Frequency is discriminated according to the site of maximum vibration on the basilar membrane.
bb154d61-ef3c-4827-a74e-7b87f35e41f6	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	610	What do the lateral olfactory stria represent?	Primary olfactory areas.
c5dbfa86-3958-48b8-8062-b16f96010e2a	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	611	What are the second-order neurons of the olfactory pathway?	Mitral and tufted cells.
1981ab44-b840-45ef-b3f7-dae33b5abd33	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	612	Where is the primary auditory cortex located?	Superior temporal gyrus, inferior lip of the lateral fissure.
800cfd90-f685-4e37-b807-e443547f10a1	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	613	What is the function of the inferior colliculus?	Center for auditory reflexes.
66fe704f-bcc1-4a1d-a929-206c34f07575	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	614	How is sound intensity discriminated by nerve impulse rate?	More intense sounds produce larger receptor potentials and increased impulse frequency.
340b4d57-8446-45a4-8cf3-7fe47885c17f	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	615	How is bone conduction affected in perceptive deafness?	Bone and ossicular conduction are equally affected.
63706117-c931-4092-8929-6bae17d63b23	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	616	Which foods are mentioned as examples of umami?	Meat extracts and aged cheese.
e916ab88-0ae5-459f-94be-1e0291365bc4	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	617	What is the normal Schwabach test result?	Examiner does not hear after the patient's bone conduction is over.
333d6d2a-30ce-4804-86ba-07f937e1b40e	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	618	What is the shape of a taste bud?	Barrel-shaped.
119105ae-a78e-4145-943a-3944cca4baeb	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	619	Where is sour taste mainly perceived?	Edges of the tongue.
622583b9-af22-493a-b8e4-4538d795be60	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	620	What type of receptors are taste receptors?	Chemoreceptors.
e6170993-66f1-45e0-b1cc-19e27a00532b	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	621	When are outer hair cells stimulated?	Only by very loud sounds.
79f9725c-80df-4a35-b034-54f3c47c84b1	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	622	Where do second-order taste fibers cross?	To the opposite side.
72d8dd72-3afe-4e2f-9f88-8bd884656eb4	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	623	Where do third-order taste fibers terminate?	Lower part of postcentral gyrus, face region.
00bc70b7-686c-4854-aee7-41e539b86211	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	624	What is the Rinne test result in nerve deafness?	Air conduction remains greater than bone conduction, but both are below normal.
4f071f3d-065a-475f-b49e-0e2c0358f6df	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	625	How does the number of stimulated hair cells indicate sound intensity?	Greater amplitude stimulates more hair cells and nerve fibers.
585095f7-22ce-4c2d-8dcd-3000ce8b6949	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	626	How many axons enter one olfactory glomerulus?	Approximately 25,000.
f1be995a-01bb-48f1-bd33-aa6e5a538ad0	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	627	What is the fourth-order neuron of the auditory pathway?	Inferior colliculus.
b83729b0-12e9-42da-a0ce-35a3ed8b0fd0	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	628	How many modified epithelial cells compose a taste bud?	Approximately 40 cells.
a3500579-24b9-4917-9437-28816a01a73a	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	629	What do olfactory cell axons form?	Unmyelinated olfactory nerves (I).
d2cf3fb5-93ba-4224-977f-018a14c761ce	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	630	What is the lifespan of olfactory receptor cells?	Approximately 60 days.
23b8f95a-2126-4bd2-a084-265ba68160c5	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	631	What does the Schwabach test compare?	Patient's bone conduction with that of a normal subject.
e9ad1450-ce42-48dc-9ac1-6c10b8c436b4	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	632	Where are olfactory cilia located?	In the mucous covering of the olfactory mucosa.
a2d94fa7-d192-4be3-a66b-3f33f21e944c	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	633	What is the pathway of the medial olfactory stria?	Crosses through the anterior commissure and ends in the opposite olfactory bulb.
6d56915f-19be-433a-8abf-2226b9d04daf	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	634	What is the Rinne test result in conductive deafness?	Bone conduction is greater than air conduction.
63fac910-ce3d-4ab8-82b7-51f19335e18a	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	635	What are the two main types of deafness?	Conductive deafness and perceptive (nerve) deafness.
1785d1e3-4fc8-4a2a-9f75-bd00991d25e1	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	636	What secretes the mucous covering of olfactory mucosa?	Microvilli of supporting cells.
c8cafd7f-ab86-4722-b3e2-a9646ac4fd0e	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	637	Approximately how many taste buds are present in humans?	900.
93e81035-356c-4395-b684-057d7fddacb0	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	638	What is an audiogram?	A plot of hearing thresholds across tested frequencies.
8ad1c5c2-f38a-4a36-a1d7-fea07eab3d87	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	639	How quickly does olfactory adaptation occur?	Usually rapidly.
d3115d86-ce10-426b-bd38-2bb3a8eca695	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	640	What is the second-order neuron of the taste pathway?	Neurons of the solitary nucleus.
f0afc115-475f-4ac3-bdd2-244823820743	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	641	What is the first-order neuron of the taste pathway?	Sensory neurons starting from taste bud receptors.
50e5ad66-235d-430c-bb89-a0087fb66175	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	642	What happens in Weber test with nerve deafness?	Better hearing in the normal ear.
4ebb353f-0f1f-45b8-8a11-e26c31453a4c	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	643	Where do second-order taste fibers ascend?	Medial lemniscus close to trigeminal lemniscus.
195c847b-ad05-4cca-9c5b-6dfdfeaf693c	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	644	With which cells do first-order olfactory neurons synapse?	Mitral and tufted cells.
a3ad5fe5-c026-46cb-9833-fdf643b9a585	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	645	What is the chemical theory of olfaction?	Each substance reacts with a specific receptor according to its chemical nature.
361c8379-d226-4bc0-8237-e484eb86f005	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	646	Where are the cell bodies of vagal taste fibers located?	Nodose ganglion.
d529fcd5-2d24-4de9-89d6-0fe7d437d0c6	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	647	Where do olfactory nerve axons terminate?	Olfactory bulb.
f9e86d62-6a6d-4808-b745-acfe001750d3	8908481e-83e0-4363-9067-6966c2df05ce	648	What does the Weber test compare?	Bone conduction between both ears.
85914054-1097-46cd-99c5-09c1e8317997	6eff150f-088b-4acd-bfa5-1a61e03ee967	20	Antibody predominantly induced by T-cell–independent activation?	A : IgM (generally no immunological memory)
950af14c-7cad-456b-9647-b1a252fd00d7	6eff150f-088b-4acd-bfa5-1a61e03ee967	21	Two types of B-cell activation?	A : T-cell dependent and T-cell independent
451d20cd-7292-4d9c-84e0-c40ef01bd475	6eff150f-088b-4acd-bfa5-1a61e03ee967	22	Principal CD4+ T helper subsets?	A : Th1, Th2, Th17, Tfh and Treg
ee997404-175b-4850-bef2-35787d8c10a4	6eff150f-088b-4acd-bfa5-1a61e03ee967	23	Fate of a DP thymocyte whose TCR recognises MHC-I?	A : Becomes a CD8+ SP cytotoxic T cell (CD4 downregulated)
3dd880d4-fd70-4ddf-94ef-c684223b1e91	6eff150f-088b-4acd-bfa5-1a61e03ee967	24	Requirement for passing positive selection?	A : DP thymocytes must recognise self-MHC with low to moderate affinity
f89f2790-f050-4a68-a486-c1bf7e45d879	6eff150f-088b-4acd-bfa5-1a61e03ee967	25	Antigens from non-replicating viruses require what to activate CD8+ T cells?	A : Cross-presentation and enhanced APC activation, often dependent on CD4+ T-cell help
3961fbe1-a294-4f8c-8417-7b7224984ceb	6eff150f-088b-4acd-bfa5-1a61e03ee967	26	Key phagocytic cells?	A : Monocytes (macrophages, dendritic cells), neutrophils and eosinophils
9125cf37-36e5-44e8-993b-287e2388020d	6eff150f-088b-4acd-bfa5-1a61e03ee967	27	Why are RBCs not classical immune cells?	A : They lack antigen-presenting capacity, cytokine synthesis and proliferative responses
71aeda1a-3e26-447c-b9c1-3f3d99cbf053	6eff150f-088b-4acd-bfa5-1a61e03ee967	28	How do RBCs influence immunity?	A : Complement interactions, immune complex transport, modulation of inflammatory mediators and nucleic acid sensing
2b91d91a-b310-457c-a149-547003066823	6eff150f-088b-4acd-bfa5-1a61e03ee967	29	Antigens involved in T-cell–dependent activation?	A : Protein antigens
8dafe86b-75a9-431d-a4e6-9e66722db8c2	6eff150f-088b-4acd-bfa5-1a61e03ee967	30	Two mechanisms of CTL-mediated killing?	A : Perforin–granzyme pathway and Fas (CD95)–FasL (CD95L) interaction
b9353649-02ff-4452-80e2-2dba2a0044cd	6eff150f-088b-4acd-bfa5-1a61e03ee967	31	Tfh cells?	A : B-cell activation and antibody production within germinal centres
63807495-2c66-4357-9899-1e14058ed798	6eff150f-088b-4acd-bfa5-1a61e03ee967	32	Three signals of T-cell activation?	A : Antigen recognition, co-stimulation and cytokines
b1e776e3-2115-49c8-a9db-e757b63f3755	6eff150f-088b-4acd-bfa5-1a61e03ee967	33	Antigens involved in T-cell–independent activation?	A : Non-protein antigens such as polysaccharides and lipopolysaccharides
1ba91856-75e6-4c18-af1c-5c5054b7e744	6eff150f-088b-4acd-bfa5-1a61e03ee967	34	Th17 cells?	A : Protect against extracellular bacteria and fungi by recruiting neutrophils
75f76b4f-519a-4c04-8e01-66db880cee10	6eff150f-088b-4acd-bfa5-1a61e03ee967	35	Role of memory B cells?	A : Persist long term and enable a faster, more robust secondary immune response
\.
