4a6af1cb	Passive and active immunity	[{"back": "\\"In a computer\\"", "front": "What does \\"in silico\\" mean in the final slide?"}, {"back": "Because the host's own immune system is fully responsible for recognising the antigen and actively mounting an effective immune response", "front": "Why is active immunity termed \\"active\\"?"}, {"back": "Before birth, primarily IgG crosses the placenta; after birth, IgA is transferred through breast milk, especially IgA-rich colostrum in the first few days after delivery", "front": "How are maternal immunoglobulins transferred to the infant across two main stages?"}, {"back": "Diphtheria antitoxin, mAbs protecting against respiratory syncytial virus (RSV), and antivenom given after snake bites", "front": "What examples of artificial passive immunisation are given in the source?"}, {"back": "Transfer of antibodies, such as immune serum or monoclonal antibodies (mAbs), from an immune individual or manufactured source to a non-immune (naïve) recipient", "front": "What is artificial passive immunity?"}, {"back": "Because the transferred antibodies are gradually degraded and no immunological memory is established", "front": "Why is protection from artificial passive immunisation temporary?"}, {"back": "Immunity where the host's immune system makes its own antibodies after encountering a germ, taking weeks to start but creating memory cells for long-term or lifelong protection", "front": "What is active immunity?"}, {"back": "Natural transfer of immune cells, artificial transfer of immune cells, and cellular transplantation for immune reconstitution", "front": "What are the three types of adoptive transfer of immunity listed?"}, {"back": "No; the full extent and functional significance of this cellular transfer in healthy infants remain incompletely defined", "front": "Is the full functional significance of maternal milk cellular transfer settled in healthy infants?"}, {"back": "Thymus transplantation and haematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HSCT)", "front": "Which two cellular transplantations are mentioned for immune reconstitution in severe immunodeficiency?"}, {"back": "It is an established treatment for certain haematological malignancies and under active investigation for solid tumours and other diseases", "front": "What is the clinical status of adoptive cell therapy according to the source?"}, {"back": "Through low stomach acidity, physical buffering, and rapid gastric emptying, with roughly 27% to 30% surviving gastric transit to reach the infant's intestines alive", "front": "How do maternal milk cells survive passage through the baby's stomach and what proportion survives?"}, {"back": "Activation of lymphocytes, production of specific antibodies, and formation of immunological memory leading to usually long-term protection", "front": "Which processes are involved in active immunity?"}, {"back": "In selected patients with thymic aplasia, as the graft provides a microenvironment for T-lymphocyte maturation and development", "front": "In which condition is thymus transplantation particularly beneficial and why?"}, {"back": "No; in both natural and artificial passive immunity, the recipient does not actively generate an immune response against the infectious agent", "front": "Does the recipient mount an active immune response to the infectious agent in passive immunity?"}, {"back": "Immunity where the body receives ready-made antibodies from another source instead of making them itself, acting instantly but fading after weeks or months because no memory cells are made", "front": "What is passive immunity?"}, {"back": "Natural (after recovering from an infection) and artificial (following vaccination)", "front": "What are the two types of active immunity listed in the source?"}, {"back": "Transfer of soluble immune factors, particularly antibodies, from mother to child, such as through colostrum (first milk) or across the placenta", "front": "What is natural passive immunity?"}, {"back": "Maternal antibodies steadily decline while the infant's own antibody production progressively increases, becoming more functional and capable of effective levels around 7–8 months of age", "front": "How do maternal antibody levels and infant antibody production change after birth?"}, {"back": "A medical procedure that removes leukocytes (WBC) or specific immune/stem cells from blood using an automated cell separator while returning red blood cells, plasma, and platelets to the body", "front": "What is leukapheresis?"}, {"back": "Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation (formerly called bone marrow transplantation; now also using peripheral blood and umbilical cord blood) to replace defective/absent HSCs with healthy donor stem cells to reconstitute haematopoietic and immune systems", "front": "What is HSCT and what are its stem cell sources mentioned in the source?"}, {"back": "Because a newborn's immune system is not fully developed at birth and matures gradually over time", "front": "Why is colostrum important for a newborn?"}, {"back": "\\"In a living thing\\"", "front": "What does \\"in vivo\\" mean in the final slide?"}, {"back": "Macrophages, granulocytes, T cells, and B cells, which may aid local immune defence and immunoregulation, especially in the gastrointestinal tract", "front": "Which immune cells are found in human milk and may transfer to the breast-fed infant?"}, {"back": "\\"Out of a living thing\\"", "front": "What does \\"ex vivo\\" mean in the final slide?"}, {"back": "\\"In glass\\"", "front": "What does \\"in vitro\\" mean in the final slide?"}, {"back": "Red blood cells, plasma, and platelets", "front": "Which blood components are returned to the body during leukapheresis?"}, {"back": "Transfer of primed or sensitised immune cells, particularly lymphocytes, from an immune individual to a recipient to confer antigen-specific immune function", "front": "What is adoptive immunity?"}, {"back": "Donor compatibility, timing of transplanta