ll destruction (haemolysis)", "front": "What disease can result from deficiency or inhibition of CD55 or CD59?"}, {"back": "Ion imbalance, osmotic swelling, leakage of contents, and lysis of susceptible cells", "front": "What consequences result from MAC pore formation in a target cell membrane?"}, {"back": "It irreversibly inactivates C1r, C1s, and MASPs", "front": "How does C1 inhibitor (C1-INH) regulate complement?"}, {"back": "Additional C3b joins existing C3 convertases to form C4b2a3b (classical/lectin) or C3bBb3b (alternative), cleaving C5 into C5a and C5b", "front": "How are C5 convertases formed in the complement pathways?"}, {"back": "By forming the membrane attack complex (MAC), creating pores in bacterial membranes—especially Gram-negative bacteria—causing them to burst", "front": "How does the complement system cause cell lysis?"}, {"back": "It helps remove antigen-antibody complexes and damaged or apoptotic cells to prevent tissue accumulation", "front": "How does complement contribute to clearance of immune complexes and dead cells?"}, {"back": "It competes with Factor B for C3b binding and acts as a cofactor for Factor I-mediated cleavage of C3b into inactive fragments", "front": "How does Factor H regulate C3b?"}, {"back": "As inactive precursors (zymogens) sequentially activated by proteolytic cleavage", "front": "How do most complement proteins circulate in plasma before activation?"}, {"back": "It is a short-lived serine protease complex that catalyses cleavage of hundreds to thousands of C3 molecules", "front": "Why is C3 convertase (C4b2a) a major amplification step?"}, {"back": "C3a, C4a, and C5a, which increase blood vessel permeability and stimulate histamine release from mast cells (with C5a also a powerful inflammatory mediator)", "front": "Which complement fragments act as anaphylatoxins and what do they do?"}, {"back": "Through a sequential, enzyme-driven cascade that recognises danger, amplifies the response, and eliminates pathogens while limiting collateral damage to host tissues", "front": "How does the complement system balance pathogen elimination and host protection?"}, {"back": "They recognise acetylated human serum albumin, DNA, and damaged cells, form complexes with MASPs, and participate in opsonisation and inflammation", "front": "Why are ficolins crucial for first-line defence?"}, {"back": "As a rare, acquired, life-threatening blood disease characterised by complement-mediated destruction of red blood cells (haemolysis) when CD55 or CD59 is deficient or inhibited", "front": "How is paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria (PNH) characterised in the lecture?"}, {"back": "Factor D", "front": "Which enzyme cleaves Factor B in both the fluid-phase C3(H2O)Bb and surface-bound C3bBb convertases?"}, {"back": "Because complement activation is enzymatically explosive, so regulatory proteins limit convertase formation and stability to protect host tissues", "front": "Why is complement regulation necessary during activation?"}, {"back": "It continuously and spontaneously generates low-level C3(H2O) in plasma independently of antibodies to detect foreign surfaces upon contact", "front": "Why is the alternative pathway \\"tick-over\\" mechanism described as a surveillance system?"}, {"back": "They increase blood vessel permeability and stimulate histamine release from mast cells", "front": "How do C3a, C4a, and C5a contribute to inflammation at the tissue level?"}, {"back": "They insert into the lipid bilayer and polymerise to form a transmembrane pore (C5b-9)", "front": "How do C9 monomers form the MAC pore?"}, {"back": "Factor I", "front": "Which protease degrades C3b into inactive fragments with Factor H acting as a cofactor?"}, {"back": "A glycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI) anchor (it is a GPI-anchored membrane glycoprotein)", "front": "What structural feature anchors CD59 (protectin) to the cell membrane?"}, {"back": "Soluble (plasma) proteins and membrane-associated (membrane-bound) proteins", "