aces", "front": "What happens when C4b exposes its reactive thioester bond?"}, {"back": "It permits partial membrane penetration and creates a scaffold for polymerisation of multiple C9 molecules", "front": "What role does C8 play in membrane attack complex (MAC) assembly?"}, {"back": "Bacteria, viruses, fungi, and parasites", "front": "Which pathogen groups have sugar patterns recognised by mannose-binding lectin (MBL)?"}, {"back": "Complement means complete, whereas compliment means praise", "front": "What is the fundamental difference between \\"complement\\" and \\"compliment\\" in the lecture's opening note?"}, {"back": "It acts as a conformer mimicking C3b structure and function and binds Factor B to form C3(H2O)Bb", "front": "What happens to C3(H2O) after it forms by spontaneous hydrolysis of C3?"}, {"back": "C6 and C7, forming the C5b-7 complex", "front": "Which components join C5b sequentially to form the membrane-inserting complex?"}, {"back": "Dying host cells and altered-self Ags", "front": "Besides pathogens, what host targets does MBL bind to facilitate clearance?"}, {"back": "It prevents the accumulation of antigen-antibody complexes and damaged or apoptotic cells in tissues", "front": "Why is clearance of immune complexes and dead cells important?"}], "color": "amber", "folder": 0}, {"name": "immune Response", "cards": [{"back": "Neutrophils, macrophages, and monocytes (phagocytosis), NK cells (virus-infected host cells or tumour cells), and eosinophils, basophils, and mast cells (inflammatory mediators, e.g., parasites and allergies)", "front": "What are the cellular defences of innate immunity in the source?"}, {"back": "A generally faster and stronger adaptive response occurring after subsequent exposure to the same antigen due to immunological memory", "front": "What is a secondary immune response?"}, {"back": "An antigen-specific defence that develops more slowly and generates immunological memory", "front": "What defines the adaptive immune response?"}, {"back": "Calor (heat), rubor (redness), tumor (swelling), dolor (pain), and functio laesa (loss of function)", "front": "What are the five classic signs of acute inflammation listed in the source?"}, {"back": "A very small number with specific receptors are activated with co-stimulatory signals, undergo clonal expansion, and differentiate into effector cells and long-lived memory B and T cells", "front": "What happens to naïve B and T lymphocytes during a primary antigen exposure?"}, {"back": "It rushes blood flow, immune cells, and healing proteins to a local injury or infection site to trap and clear out germs", "front": "How does acute inflammation help isolate damage?"}, {"back": "Primary immune response and secondary immune response", "front": "How is the adaptive immune response subdivided by time of exposure to antigens?"}, {"back": "Because protection against infection and disease relies on their coordinated, regulated, and integrated actions", "front": "Why must innate and adaptive immunity cooperate?"}, {"back": "Lag (latent) phase, log (exponential) phase, plateau (steady-state) phase, and decline phase", "front": "What are the four phases of the primary antibody response?"}, {"back": "Typically 4–7 days with no detectable serum antibody while antigen recognition, clonal expansion, and differentiation occur", "front": "How long is the lag (latent) phase and what occurs during it?"}, {"back": "Skin and mucous membranes, gastric acid (low pH), and secretions (saliva, tears, and mucous containing enzymes such as lysozyme)", "front": "What are the physical and chemical barriers of innate immunity in the source?"}, {"back": "Germline-encoded host sensor proteins expressed by immune cells (macrophages, dendritic cells, neutrophils) and non-immune cells (epithelial cells)", "front": "What are pattern recognition receptors (PRRs)?"}, {"back": "Inflammation and fever", "front": "Which two physiological responses are listed under innate immunity?"}, {"ba