ise cytokine networks and activity?"}, {"back": "CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes", "front": "Which T cells recognise MHC-I-peptide complexes on the cell surface?"}, {"back": "They remain CD3- and differentiate into other lymphocyte lineages such as B cells or NK cells", "front": "What happens to lymphoid precursors that do not follow the thymic TCR-CD3 pathway?"}, {"back": "Transporter associated with antigen processing (TAP)", "front": "Which transporter moves peptides from the cytosol into the ER lumen in the endogenous pathway?"}, {"back": "Antibodies, TCR, MHC molecules, CD3, CD4, CD8, Costimulatory Receptor (CD28), VCAM-1, and ICAMs", "front": "List examples of IgSF members from the source."}, {"back": "Activation of naïve CD8+ T cells by APCs that have presented exogenous antigens on MHC-I molecules", "front": "What is cross-priming?"}, {"back": "Both use V(D)J recombination and junctional diversity, but BCR also undergoes somatic hypermutation and class switching, which TCR lacks", "front": "How do diversity generation mechanisms compare between BCR and TCR?"}, {"back": "Phagoendolysosomes", "front": "What forms when phagosomes fuse with late endosomes and lysosomes?"}, {"back": "Selective uptake via receptors forming membrane-bound vesicles to transport materials inside", "front": "What is receptor-mediated endocytosis?"}, {"back": "TCR α and β chains confer antigen recognition, whereas CD3 γ and δ chains function solely in signal transduction", "front": "Why must TCR α and β chains not be confused with CD3 γ and δ chains?"}, {"back": "\\"Cell eating\\" of large particles via membrane-bound vesicles", "front": "What is phagocytosis?"}, {"back": "Assists in exchanging CLIP for antigenic peptides", "front": "What is the role of HLA-DM in the MHC-II pathway?"}, {"back": "TCRs recognise only processed peptide fragments presented by MHC molecules, whereas BCRs recognise native (unprocessed) antigens", "front": "How does TCR antigen recognition differ from BCR antigen recognition?"}, {"back": "The proteasome", "front": "Which structure degrades endogenous proteins into short peptide fragments?"}, {"back": "Cross-presentation is displaying exogenous antigens on MHC-I, whereas cross-priming is the resulting activation of antigen-specific CD8+ T cells", "front": "How do cross-presentation and cross-priming differ?"}, {"back": "Igα (CD79a) and Igβ (CD79b) for BCR; CD3 complex (CD3γ, CD3δ, CD3ε) and ζ chains for TCR", "front": "What signalling molecules associate with BCR versus TCR?"}, {"back": "By acting on the hypothalamus", "front": "How do IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-α induce fever in the APR?"}, {"back": "8-10 amino acids", "front": "What is the typical length of peptides generated by the proteasome in the endogenous pathway?"}, {"back": "C-reactive protein (CRP) and serum amyloid A (SAA)", "front": "Which acute phase proteins are synthesised by the liver during the APR?"}, {"back": "Exogenous Pathway, Endogenous Pathway, and Cross-Presentation Pathway", "front": "What are the three principal antigen processing pathways?"}, {"back": "Approximately 13-25 amino acids", "front": "What is the approximate length of peptides produced by protein degradation in endolysosomes and phagoendolysosomes?"}, {"back": "IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-α", "front": "Which pro-inflammatory cytokines primarily orchestrate the APR?"}, {"back": "Autoimmune conditions, chronic inflammation, infections, and cancer", "front": "What can imbalanced cytokine production or signalling drive?"}, {"back": "In the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) with the invariant chain (Ii)", "front": "Where are MHC-II molecules synthesised and with which chain?"}, {"back": "A specialised Ag-processing pathway where certain professional APCs, particularly dendritic cells, present exogenous Ags on MHC-I molecules", "front": "What is cross-presentation?"}, {"back": "\\"Cell drinking\\" of fluid and solutes via membrane-bound vesicles", "front": "What is pinocytosis?"}, {"back": "Phagocytosis, receptor