nd a smaller soluble fragment (\\"a\\") often with pro-inflammatory activity", "front": "What are the two fragments typically produced by proteolytic cleavage of complement proteins?"}, {"back": "Opsonisation, cell lysis, inflammation, chemotaxis, clearance of immune complexes and dead cells, and enhancement of adaptive immunity", "front": "What are the six main functions of the complement system?"}, {"back": "Classical, lectin, and alternative pathways, which converge on common enzymatic steps, notably C3 and C5 cleavage by convertases", "front": "What are the three complement activation pathways and where do they converge?"}, {"back": "Primarily mannose, N-acetyl-D-glucosamine, fucose, and glucose on pathogens, plus dying host cells and altered-self Ags", "front": "Which carbohydrates does MBL bind and what else does it recognise?"}, {"back": "C1q plus two molecules each of the serine proteases C1r and C1s", "front": "What is the composition of the calcium-dependent C1 complex?"}, {"back": "C5b sequentially binds C6 and C7 to form membrane-inserting C5b-7, then binds C8 for partial membrane penetration and C9 polymerisation into a transmembrane pore (C5b-9)", "front": "How does MAC assemble starting from C5b?"}, {"back": "It induces autoactivation of C1r, which cleaves and activates C1s, initiating the cascade by cleaving C4 and C2", "front": "What happens when C1q binds antibody Fc regions?"}, {"back": "A complex network of plasma and membrane-associated proteins forming a major effector arm of innate immunity and an essential amplifier of adaptive immune responses", "front": "What is the complement system (CS)?"}, {"back": "It acts as both an independent activation route and a powerful amplification loop for C3b generated by classical and lectin pathways", "front": "What dual role does the alternative pathway play?"}, {"back": "Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria (PNH), a rare, acquired, life-threatening blood disorder marked by complement-mediated red blood ce