ing on exposure site and nature)—while IgM changes relatively little", "front": "Which antibodies predominate in the secondary response and how do IgM levels change?"}, {"back": "IFN-γ, TGF-β, and several interleukins, which influence the antibody class produced", "front": "What regulates Ig isotype (class) switching?"}, {"back": "Th1-derived IL-2 and Th2-derived cytokines such as IL-4, IL-5, IL-6, IL-10, and IL-13", "front": "Which cytokines drive B-cell proliferation and differentiation?"}, {"back": "It processes the bacterium via the exogenous pathway, loads peptide fragments onto MHC-II molecules, and transports peptide–MHC-II complexes to the APC surface for presentation to CD4+ helper T cells", "front": "How does an APC process a phagocytosed bacterium and present it to CD4+ helper T cells?"}, {"back": "Excessive pathway activation regulates cell-proliferation genes (e.g., BCL2 and c-Myc with high STAT3 activity); overactive STAT3 is linked to higher melanoma recurrence risk post-treatment, and high STAT5 activity is associated with higher mortality likelihood in prostate cancer", "front": "How is elevated STAT3 or STAT5 activity linked to cancer progression and prognosis?"}, {"back": "Complement fixation and phagocytosis, antigen processing and presentation and CD4+ Th-cell activation, B-cell activation, B-cell proliferation and differentiation (plasma and memory cells), and antibody production", "front": "What are the five steps of cellular interactions for antibody synthesis in the source?"}, {"back": "Myeloproliferative neoplasms (polycythemia vera [PV], essential thrombocythemia [ET], myelofibrosis [MF]), rheumatoid arthritis, atopic dermatitis, and multiple malignancies", "front": "Which diseases are associated with JAK-STAT dysregulation, mutations, or persistent activation?"}, {"back": "They undergo clonal proliferation and differentiate into plasma cells capable of Ig isotype switching", "front": "What happens to B cells after activation?"}, {"back": 