ne response and mediated by phagocytic cells", "front": "How is antibody production initiated in response to T-dependent antigens?"}, {"back": "A fundamental mechanism transmitting extracellular signals from cytokines and growth factors directly to the nucleus to regulate gene expression", "front": "What is the JAK-STAT signalling pathway?"}, {"back": "Cross-linking of surface immunoglobulin (B-cell receptor, BCR) by foreign antigens", "front": "What is the first signal required for B-cell activation?"}, {"back": "Immune system regulation, cell growth and proliferation, cell differentiation, cell death (apoptosis), and tissue repair", "front": "Which cellular processes involve the JAK-STAT pathway according to the source?"}, {"back": "JAK activation, where receptor dimerisation brings associated JAKs together to autophosphorylate each other and phosphorylate the receptor", "front": "What is Step 2 of the JAK-STAT signalling cascade?"}, {"back": "Signal binding of a cytokine or ligand to its cell-surface receptor, inducing receptor dimerisation (or conformational rearrangement bringing subunits into close proximity)", "front": "What is Step 1 of the JAK-STAT signalling cascade?"}, {"back": "It drives resting B cells into the cell cycle, is essential for T-dependent antigen responses, and is required for immunoglobulin isotype (class) switching and memory B-cell generation", "front": "What are the roles of CD40–CD40L (CD154) interaction on B cells?"}, {"back": "STAT dimerisation, where phosphorylated STATs form homodimers (two identical STATs) or heterodimers (two different STATs)", "front": "What is Step 4 of the JAK-STAT signalling cascade?"}, {"back": "Transcription factors activated downstream of JAK signalling: STAT1, STAT2, STAT3, STAT4, STAT5A, STAT5B, and STAT6", "front": "What are STATs and what are the seven STAT proteins?"}, {"back": "It is crucial for innate and adaptive immune responses (inflammation, cell differentiation, pathogen defence), and dysregula