-mediated endocytosis, or pinocytosis", "front": "What mechanisms capture antigens in the exogenous pathway?"}, {"back": "No; it binds directly to free or surface antigens (proteins, carbohydrates, lipids, nucleic acids, whole pathogens), whereas TCR requires MHC", "front": "Does BCR require MHC for antigen recognition?"}, {"back": "Immune recognition, cell-cell interaction, adhesion, and signalling", "front": "What roles do IgSF members play?"}, {"back": "Endolysosomes", "front": "What forms when acidifying endosomes fuse with lysosomes after antigen internalisation?"}, {"back": "Conventional type 1 dendritic cells (cDC1s)", "front": "Which dendritic cell subset is particularly efficient at cross-presentation?"}, {"back": "The CLIP (class II-associated invariant chain peptide) fragment", "front": "What remains after invariant chain (Ii) degradation on MHC-II?"}, {"back": "Immune surveillance of intracellular infections and malignant transformation, essential for CTL-mediated immune defence", "front": "What is the role of the endogenous pathway?"}, {"back": "B cells differentiate into antibody-producing plasma cells (humoral immunity); T cells differentiate into helper, cytotoxic, regulatory, or memory T cells (cell-mediated immunity)", "front": "What are the effector outcomes of B-cell versus T-cell activation?"}, {"back": "CD4+ helper T cells", "front": "Which T cells recognise peptide–MHC-II complexes on the cell surface?"}, {"back": "Increases a thousandfold (stimulated in the liver by IL-1), serving as a non-specific indicator of inflammation", "front": "How does CRP change within 24 to 48 hours of acute inflammation onset?"}, {"back": "One CD3γ chain, one CD3δ chain, two CD3ε chains, and two CD3ζ chains", "front": "What are the six polypeptide chains composing the CD3 complex?"}, {"back": "No; TCR is always membrane-bound, whereas BCR can be secreted as antibodies after B-cell activation", "front": "Is TCR secreted after activation?"}, {"back": "Facilitation of TCR surface expression and intracellular signal transduction (with ζ chain central to signal transduction), without contributing to antigen-binding specificity", "front": "What are the two main functions of CD3?"}, {"back": "Professional APCs including dendritic cells, macrophages, and B cells", "front": "Which cells capture antigens in the exogenous pathway?"}, {"back": "Antigens originating within the cytosol, including viral proteins from intracellular infection, tumour-associated Ags, and self-proteins undergoing normal turnover", "front": "What antigens are processed by the endogenous pathway?"}], "color": "violet", "folder": 0}, {"name": "Complete system", "cards": [{"back": "L-, M-, and H-ficolin; they recognise acetylated human serum albumin, DNA, and damaged cells, and aid opsonisation and inflammation", "front": "What are the human ficolin types and what do they recognise?"}, {"back": "C1s cleaves C4 into C4a and surface-bound C4b, which binds C2; C1s then cleaves C2 into C2a and C2b, leaving C4b2a", "front": "How is the classical (and lectin) pathway C3 convertase formed?"}, {"back": "Complement fragments help activate B cells and improve antibody production, linking innate and adaptive immunity", "front": "How does complement enhance adaptive immunity?"}, {"back": "A single pentameric IgM molecule or multiple IgG molecules in close proximity", "front": "How much IgM or IgG is sufficient to activate the C1 complex?"}, {"back": "Continuous, spontaneous, low-level activation in plasma via hydrolysis of native C3's internal thioester bond by H2O to form C3(H2O), independent of antibodies", "front": "What is the \\"tick-over\\" mechanism of the alternative pathway?"}, {"back": "Antibody-antigen (Ab-Ag) immune complexes containing IgM or IgG (particularly IgG1 and IgG3)", "front": "What triggers the classical pathway?"}, {"back": "By coating microorganisms so phagocytes like neutrophils and macrophages can more easily recognise and engulf them", "front": "How does C3b mediate opsonisation?"}, {"back": "Nascent C3b binds microbial surfaces lacking regulators via its thioester bond, binds Factor B (cleaved by Factor D) to form C3bBb, and is stabilised by properdin (Factor P)", "front": "How is the surface-bound alternative C3 convertase formed and stabilised?"}, {"back": "It is a GPI-anchored membrane glycoprotein that binds C8 and C9 to prevent C9 polymerisation, MAC formation, and cell lysis", "front": "How does CD59 (protectin) protect host cells from MAC?"}, {"back": "MASP-1 and MASP-2 (analogous to C1r and C1s); upon binding, MASP-2 activates and cleaves C4 and C2 like C1s to form C4b2a", "front": "Which proteases associate with MBL and ficolins and what does MASP-2 do?"}, {"back": "Approximately 1% of circulating C3 per hour, generating C3(H2O), which mimics the structure and function of C3b", "front": "At what rate does C3 undergo spontaneous hydrolysis in circulation and what does C3(H2O) mimic?"}, {"back": "It is antibody-independent and initiated by pattern recognition molecules—primarily mannose-binding lectin (MBL) and ficolins—binding conserved microbial carbohydrates", "front": "How is the lectin pathway initiated and is it antibody-dependent?"}, {"back": "It promotes dissociation of both classical and alternative pathway convertases", "front": "What is the role of decay-accelerating factor (CD55)?"}, {"back": "It attracts neutrophils, monocytes, and other immune cells to the site of infection to enhance inflammation", "front": "What is the chemotactic role of C5a?"}, {"back": "More than 30 soluble and membrane-bound proteins, including C1-C9 and regulators such as Factor H and MBL", "front": "How many proteins are in the complement system?"}, {"back": "C3(H2O) binds Factor B, which is cleaved by Factor D to form C3(H2O)Bb, cleaving native C3 into C3a and C3b", "front": "How is the fluid-phase C3 convertase formed in the alternative pathway?"}, {"back": "A larger fragment (\\"b\\") binding target surfaces and a smaller soluble fragment (\\"a\\") often with pro-inflammatory activity", "front": "What are the two fragments typically produced by proteolytic cleavage of complement proteins?"}, {"back": "Opsonisation, cell lysis, inflammation, chemotaxis, clearance of immune complexes and dead cells, and enhancement of adaptive immunity", "front": "What are the six main functions of the complement system?"}, {"back": "Classical, lectin, and alternative pathways, which converge on common enzymatic steps, notably C3 and C5 cleavage by convertases", "front": "What are the three complement activation pathways and where do they converge?"}, {"back": "Primarily mannose, N-acetyl-D-glucosamine, fucose, and glucose on pathogens, plus dying host cells and altered-self Ags", "front": "Which carbohydrates does MBL bind and what else does it recognise?"}, {"back": "C1q plus two molecules each of the serine proteases C1r and C1s", "front": "What is the composition of the calcium-dependent C1 complex?"}, {"back": "C5b sequentially binds C6 and C7 to form membrane-inserting C5b-7, then binds C8 for partial membrane penetration and C9 polymerisation into a transmembrane pore (C5b-9)", "front": "How does MAC assemble starting from C5b?"}, {"back": "It induces autoactivation of C1r, which cleaves and activates C1s, initiating the cascade by cleaving C4 and C2", "front": "What happens when C1q binds antibody Fc regions?"}, {"back": "A complex network of plasma and membrane-associated proteins forming a major effector arm of innate immunity and an essential amplifier of adaptive immune responses", "front": "What is the complement system (CS)?"}, {"back": "It acts as both an independent activation route and a powerful amplification loop for C3b generated by classical and lectin pathways", "front": "What dual role does the alternative pathway play?"}, {"back": "Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria (PNH), a rare, acquired, life-threatening blood disorder marked by complement-mediated red blood cell destruction (haemolysis)", "front": "What disease can result from deficiency or inhibition of CD55 or CD59?"}, {"back": "Ion imbalance, osmotic swelling, leakage of contents, and lysis of susceptible cells", "front": "What consequences result from MAC pore formation in a target cell membrane?"}, {"back": "It irreversibly inactivates C1r, C1s, and MASPs", "front": "How does C1 inhibitor (C1-INH) regulate complement?"}, {"back": "Additional C3b joins existing C3 convertases to form C4b2a3b (classical/lectin) or C3bBb3b (alternative), cleaving C5 into C5a and C5b", "front": "How are C5 convertases formed in the complement pathways?"}, {"back": "By forming the membrane attack complex (MAC), creating pores in bacterial membranes—especially Gram-negative bacteria—causing them to burst", "front": "How does the complement system cause cell lysis?"}, {"back": "It helps remove antigen-antibody complexes and damaged or apoptotic cells to prevent tissue accumulation", "front": "How does complement contribute to clearance of immune complexes and dead cells?"}, {"back": "It competes with Factor B for C3b binding and acts as a cofactor for Factor I-mediated cleavage of C3b into inactive fragments", "front": "How does Factor H regulate C3b?"}, {"back": "As inactive precursors (zymogens) sequentially activated by proteolytic cleavage", "front": "How do most complement proteins circulate in plasma before activation?"}, {"back": "It is a short-lived serine protease complex that catalyses cleavage of hundreds to thousands of C3 molecules", "front": "Why is C3 convertase (C4b2a) a major amplification step?"}, {"back": "C3a, C4a, and C5a, which increase blood vessel permeability and stimulate histamine release from mast cells (with C5a also a powerful inflammatory mediator)", "front": "Which complement fragments act as anaphylatoxins and what do they do?"}, {"back": "Through a sequential, enzyme-driven cascade that recognises danger, amplifies the response, and eliminates pathogens while limiting collateral damage to host tissues", "front": "How does the complement system balance pathogen elimination and host protection?"}, {"back": "They recognise acetylated human serum albumin, DNA, and damaged cells, form complexes with MASPs, and participate in opsonisation and inflammation", "front": "Why are ficolins crucial for first-line defence?"}, {"back": "As a rare, acquired, life-threatening blood disease characterised by complement-mediated destruction of red blood cells (haemolysis) when CD55 or CD59 is deficient or inhibited", "front": "How is paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria (PNH) characterised in the lecture?"}, {"back": "Factor D", "front": "Which enzyme cleaves Factor B in both the fluid-phase C3(H2O)Bb and surface-bound C3bBb convertases?"}, {"back": "Because complement activation is enzymatically explosive, so regulatory proteins limit convertase formation and stability to protect host tissues", "front": "Why is complement regulation necessary during activation?"}, {"back": "It continuously and spontaneously generates low-level C3(H2O) in plasma independently of antibodies to detect foreign surfaces upon contact", "front": "Why is the alternative pathway \\"tick-over\\" mechanism described as a surveillance system?"}, {"back": "They increase blood vessel permeability and stimulate histamine release from mast cells", "front": "How do C3a, C4a, and C5a contribute to inflammation at the tissue level?"}, {"back": "They insert into the lipid bilayer and polymerise to form a transmembrane pore (C5b-9)", "front": "How do C9 monomers form the MAC pore?"}, {"back": "Factor I", "front": "Which protease degrades C3b into inactive fragments with Factor H acting as a cofactor?"}, {"back": "A glycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI) anchor (it is a GPI-anchored membrane glycoprotein)", "front": "What structural feature anchors CD59 (protectin) to the cell membrane?"}, {"back": "Soluble (plasma) proteins and membrane-associated (membrane-bound) proteins", "front": "What are the two main forms of proteins making up the complement system?"}, {"back": "C4b2a3b", "front": "Which convertase cleaves C5 into C5a and C5b in the classical and lectin pathways?"}, {"back": "Because microbial surfaces lack complement regulatory proteins that limit convertase formation and stability", "front": "Why is C3bBb formed preferentially on microbial surfaces rather than host surfaces?"}, {"back": "C2a (while C2b is the smaller cleaved fragment)", "front": "Which fragment of C2 remains associated with C4b to form the classical and lectin C3 convertase?"}, {"back": "IgM or IgG (particularly IgG1 and IgG3) within antibody-antigen immune complexes", "front": "Which antibody isotypes trigger the classical pathway?"}, {"back": "C1r, C1s, and MASPs", "front": "Which proteases are irreversibly inactivated by C1 inhibitor (C1-INH)?"}, {"back": "Because a single C4b2a complex catalyses the cleavage of hundreds to thousands of C3 molecules", "front": "Why does the classical and lectin C3 convertase produce large-scale amplification?"}, {"back": "C3bBb3b", "front": "Which convertase cleaves C5 into C5a and C5b in the alternative pathway?"}, {"back": "MASP-2 (which cleaves C4 and C2 identically to activated C1s)", "front": "Which MBL-associated serine protease is activated upon MBL or ficolin binding to microbial carbohydrates?"}, {"back": "It binds and stabilises C3bBb, prolonging its half-life and markedly enhancing C3 cleavage", "front": "How does properdin (Factor P) affect C3bBb?"}, {"back": "An opsonin fragment shown alongside C3b and ficolins in the complement functions diagram", "front": "Which complement fragment is designated iC3b in the overview diagram?"}, {"back": "C5a and C3a (alongside C4a in the inflammation slide)", "front": "Which complement fragments are listed as chemoattractants/anaphylatoxins in the functions diagram?"}, {"back": "It allows covalent attachment to nearby hydroxyl or amino groups on microbial or immune complex surfaces", "front": "What happens when C4b exposes its reactive thioester bond?"}, {"back": "It permits partial membrane penetration and creates a scaffold for polymerisation of multiple C9 molecules", "front": "What role does C8 play in membrane attack complex (MAC) assembly?"}, {"back": "Bacteria, viruses, fungi, and parasites", "front": "Which pathogen groups have sugar patterns recognised by mannose-binding lectin (MBL)?"}, {"back": "Complement means complete, whereas compliment means praise", "front": "What is the fundamental difference between \\"complement\\" and \\"compliment\\" in the lecture's opening note?"}, {"back": "It acts as a conformer mimicking C3b structure and function and binds Factor B to form C3(H2O)Bb", "front": "What happens to C3(H2O) after it forms by spontaneous hydrolysis of C3?"}, {"back": "C6 and C7, forming the C5b-7 complex", "front": "Which components join C5b sequentially to form the membrane-inserting complex?"}, {"back": "Dying host cells and altered-self Ags", "front": "Besides pathogens, what host targets does MBL bind to facilitate clearance?"}, {"back": "It prevents the accumulation of antigen-antibody complexes and damaged or apoptotic cells in tissues", "front": "Why is clearance of immune complexes and dead cells important?"}], "color": "amber", "folder": 0}, {"name": "immune Response", "cards": [{"back": "Neutrophils, macrophages, and monocytes (phagocytosis), NK cells (virus-infected host cells or tumour cells), and eosinophils, basophils, and mast cells (inflammatory mediators, e.g., parasites and allergies)", "front": "What are the cellular defences of innate immunity in the source?"}, {"back": "A generally faster and stronger adaptive response occurring after subsequent exposure to the same antigen due to immunological memory", "front": "What is a secondary immune response?"}, {"back": "An antigen-specific defence that develops more slowly and generates immunological memory", "front": "What defines the adaptive immune response?"}, {"back": "Calor (heat), rubor (redness), tumor (swelling), dolor (pain), and functio laesa (loss of function)", "front": "What are the five classic signs of acute inflammation listed in the source?"}, {"back": "A very small number with specific receptors are activated with co-stimulatory signals, undergo clonal expansion, and differentiate into effector cells and long-lived memory B and T cells", "front": "What happens to naïve B and T lymphocytes during a primary antigen exposure?"}, {"back": "It rushes blood flow, immune cells, and healing proteins to a local injury or infection site to trap and clear out germs", "front": "How does acute inflammation help isolate damage?"}, {"back": "Primary immune response and secondary immune response", "front": "How is the adaptive immune response subdivided by time of exposure to antigens?"}, {"back": "Because protection against infection and disease relies on their coordinated, regulated, and integrated actions", "front": "Why must innate and adaptive immunity cooperate?"}, {"back": "Lag (latent) phase, log (exponential) phase, plateau (steady-state) phase, and decline phase", "front": "What are the four phases of the primary antibody response?"}, {"back": "Typically 4–7 days with no detectable serum antibody while antigen recognition, clonal expansion, and differentiation occur", "front": "How long is the lag (latent) phase and what occurs during it?"}, {"back": "Skin and mucous membranes, gastric acid (low pH), and secretions (saliva, tears, and mucous containing enzymes such as lysozyme)", "front": "What are the physical and chemical barriers of innate immunity in the source?"}, {"back": "Germline-encoded host sensor proteins expressed by immune cells (macrophages, dendritic cells, neutrophils) and non-immune cells (epithelial cells)", "front": "What are pattern recognition receptors (PRRs)?"}, {"back": "Inflammation and fever", "front": "Which two physiological responses are listed under innate immunity?"}, {"back": "Markedly shorter, typically 1–4 days, due to pre-existing memory B cells", "front": "How long is the lag phase in the secondary (anamnestic) immune response?"}, {"back": "High Ag specificity, extensive receptor diversity, clonal expansion, and immunological memory mediated by B cells and T cells", "front": "What are the key characteristics of adaptive immunity?"}, {"back": "The log phase is faster and steeper, the plateau reaches a much higher peak (often 100–1000 times higher), and the decline phase is more sustained and slower", "front": "How do the log, plateau, and decline phases differ in a secondary response compared with a primary response?"}, {"back": "It raises core body temperature, creating a hostile environment that slows the replication of many bacteria and viruses", "front": "How does fever help fight pathogens?"}, {"back": "IgM is produced first, followed by lower levels of IgG, with lower average affinity than in a secondary response because affinity maturation is still ongoing", "front": "Which antibody isotype appears first in the primary response and how does its affinity compare?"}, {"back": "Primary exposure may cause noticeable illness, whereas secondary exposure is often milder or prevented altogether", "front": "How do symptoms typically compare between primary and secondary immune responses?"}, {"back": "Higher temperatures speed up cellular metabolism, help white blood cells (such as T-cells and neutrophils) work faster and more efficiently, and increase antibody production", "front": "How does fever boost immune cells?"}, {"back": "Activated B cells differentiate into antibody-secreting plasma cells, causing a rapid rise in serum antibody concentration that typically peaks in 7–10 days", "front": "What happens in the log (exponential) phase and when does antibody titer typically peak?"}, {"back": "A rapid, non-specific defence that provides the initial response to infection", "front": "What defines the innate immune response?"}, {"back": "A slower and weaker adaptive response occurring after the first exposure to an antigen", "front": "What is a primary immune response?"}, {"back": "Humoral immunity (mediated primarily by B cells and antibodies) and cell-mediated immunity (mediated primarily by T cells)", "front": "How is adaptive immunity subdivided by effector mechanism?"}, {"back": "Innate immune response (natural) and adaptive immune response (acquired)", "front": "What are the two major types of immune response?"}, {"back": "Relatively stable peak antibody levels reflecting a balance between antibody synthesis and catabolism (decay)", "front": "What defines the plateau (steady-state) phase?"}, {"back": "How the body recognises and defends itself against harmful microorganisms (parasites, bacteria, and viruses), their substances such as toxins, foreign materials, and abnormal cells (including tumour cells)", "front": "What is the immune response?"}, {"back": "Antibody levels gradually fall as short-lived plasma cells undergo apoptosis and antibodies are cleared after pathogen elimination", "front": "What occurs during the decline phase?"}, {"back": "Complement system provides opsonisation and MAC; cytokines act in signalling to coordinate cell movement, boost inflammation, and warn neighbouring cells of an attack", "front": "What roles do the complement system and cytokines play in innate immunity?"}, {"back": "Innate immunity initiates and activates adaptive responses via APCs (such as dendritic cells) and cytokines, while adaptive responses (such as antibodies) enhance innate functions like complement activation and phagocytosis", "front": "How do innate and adaptive immunity link together?"}, {"back": "Due to affinity maturation driven by somatic hypermutation and selection of high-affinity B-cell clones in germinal centres", "front": "Why do antibodies in a secondary response have higher affinity?"}, {"back": "Class-switched antibodies predominate—most commonly IgG (or IgA or IgE depending on exposure site and nature)—while IgM changes relatively little", "front": "Which antibodies predominate in the secondary response and how do IgM levels change?"}, {"back": "IFN-γ, TGF-β, and several interleukins, which influence the antibody class produced", "front": "What regulates Ig isotype (class) switching?"}, {"back": "Th1-derived IL-2 and Th2-derived cytokines such as IL-4, IL-5, IL-6, IL-10, and IL-13", "front": "Which cytokines drive B-cell proliferation and differentiation?"}, {"back": "It processes the bacterium via the exogenous pathway, loads peptide fragments onto MHC-II molecules, and transports peptide–MHC-II complexes to the APC surface for presentation to CD4+ helper T cells", "front": "How does an APC process a phagocytosed bacterium and present it to CD4+ helper T cells?"}, {"back": "Excessive pathway activation regulates cell-proliferation genes (e.g., BCL2 and c-Myc with high STAT3 activity); overactive STAT3 is linked to higher melanoma recurrence risk post-treatment, and high STAT5 activity is associated with higher mortality likelihood in prostate cancer", "front": "How is elevated STAT3 or STAT5 activity linked to cancer progression and prognosis?"}, {"back": "Complement fixation and phagocytosis, antigen processing and presentation and CD4+ Th-cell activation, B-cell activation, B-cell proliferation and differentiation (plasma and memory cells), and antibody production", "front": "What are the five steps of cellular interactions for antibody synthesis in the source?"}, {"back": "Myeloproliferative neoplasms (polycythemia vera [PV], essential thrombocythemia [ET], myelofibrosis [MF]), rheumatoid arthritis, atopic dermatitis, and multiple malignancies", "front": "Which diseases are associated with JAK-STAT dysregulation, mutations, or persistent activation?"}, {"back": "They undergo clonal proliferation and differentiate into plasma cells capable of Ig isotype switching", "front": "What happens to B cells after activation?"}, {"back": 