t": "What is artificial passive immunity?"}, {"back": "Because the transferred antibodies are gradually degraded and no immunological memory is established", "front": "Why is protection from artificial passive immunisation temporary?"}, {"back": "Immunity where the host's immune system makes its own antibodies after encountering a germ, taking weeks to start but creating memory cells for long-term or lifelong protection", "front": "What is active immunity?"}, {"back": "Natural transfer of immune cells, artificial transfer of immune cells, and cellular transplantation for immune reconstitution", "front": "What are the three types of adoptive transfer of immunity listed?"}, {"back": "No; the full extent and functional significance of this cellular transfer in healthy infants remain incompletely defined", "front": "Is the full functional significance of maternal milk cellular transfer settled in healthy infants?"}, {"back": "Thymus transplantation and haematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HSCT)", "front": "Which two cellular transplantations are mentioned for immune reconstitution in severe immunodeficiency?"}, {"back": "It is an established treatment for certain haematological malignancies and under active investigation for solid tumours and other diseases", "front": "What is the clinical status of adoptive cell therapy according to the source?"}, {"back": "Through low stomach acidity, physical buffering, and rapid gastric emptying, with roughly 27% to 30% surviving gastric transit to reach the infant's intestines alive", "front": "How do maternal milk cells survive passage through the baby's stomach and what proportion survives?"}, {"back": "Activation of lymphocytes, production of specific antibodies, and formation of immunological memory leading to usually long-term protection", "front": "Which processes are involved in active immunity?"}, {"back": "In selected patients with thymic aplasia, as the graft provides a microenvironment for T-lymphocyte maturation and development", "front": "In which condition is thymus transplantation particularly beneficial and why?"}, {"back": "No; in both natural and artificial passive immunity, the recipient does not actively generate an immune response against the infectious agent", "front": "Does the recipient mount an active immune response to the infectious agent in passive immunity?"}, {"back": "Immunity where the body receives ready-made antibodies from another source instead of making them itself, acting instantly but fading after weeks or months because no memory cells are made", "front": "What is passive immunity?"}, {"back": "Natural (after recovering from an infection) and artificial (following vaccination)", "front": "What are the two types of active immunity listed in the source?"}, {"back": "Transfer of soluble immune factors, particularly antibodies, from mother to child, such as through colostrum (first milk) or across the placenta", "front": "What is natural passive immunity?"}, {"back": "Maternal antibodies steadily decline while the infant's own antibody production progressively increases, becoming more functional and capable of effective levels around 7–8 months of age", "front": "How do maternal antibody levels and infant antibody production change after birth?"}, {"back": "A medical procedure that removes leukocytes (WBC) or specific immune/stem cells from blood using an automated cell separator while returning red blood cells, plasma, and platelets to the body", "front": "What is leukapheresis?"}, {"back": "Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation (formerly called bone marrow transplantation; now also using peripheral blood and umbilical cord blood) to replace defective/absent HSCs with healthy donor stem cells to reconstitute haematopoietic and immune systems", "front": "What is HSCT and what are its stem cell sources mentioned in the source?"}, {"back": "Because a newborn's immune system is not fully developed at birth and matures gradually over time", "front": "Why is colostrum important for a newborn?"}, {"back": "\\"In a living thing\\"", "front": "What does \\"in vivo\\" mean in the final slide?"}, {"back": "Macrophages, granulocytes, T cells, and B cells, which may aid local immune defence and immunoregulation, especially in the gastrointestinal tract", "front": "Which immune cells are found in human milk and may transfer to the breast-fed infant?"}, {"back": "\\"Out of a living thing\\"", "front": "What does \\"ex vivo\\" mean in the final slide?"}, {"back": "\\"In glass\\"", "front": "What does \\"in vitro\\" mean in the final slide?"}, {"back": "Red blood cells, plasma, and platelets", "front": "Which blood components are returned to the body during leukapheresis?"}, {"back": "Transfer of primed or sensitised immune cells, particularly lymphocytes, from an immune individual to a recipient to confer antigen-specific immune function", "front": "What is adoptive immunity?"}, {"back": "Donor compatibility, timing of transplantation, risk of graft-versus-host disease (GvHD), conditioning regimen, and the patient's clinical condition", "front": "Which factors influence successful HSCT outcomes?"}, {"back": "An immunotherapeutic strategy where immune effector cells are collected, expanded and/or genetically modified ex vivo, then reinfused into the patient to enhance immune responses", "front": "What is adoptive cell therapy (ACT)?"}], "color": "blue", "folder": 0}], "folders": [{"icon": "stethoscope", "name": "Micro", "color": "blue", "parent": null}]}	2026-11-27 01:05:07.583994+00	folder	e30b86c4-7515-46e7-bcda-f3b139361e87	\N	1ee99020-5a2f-4701-bdf1-ec41b9d24eac
079024ac	Micro	{"decks": [{"name": "intro", "cards": [{"back": "A: Yolk sac (weeks 3–5 of gestation).", "front": "First site of haematopoiesis in early embryogenesis?"}, {"back": "A: Afferent lymphatic vessels.", "front": "Vessels through which lymph enters a lymph node?"}, {"back": "A: Peyer’s patches and mesenteric lymph nodes.", "front": "Examples of gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT)?"}, {"back": "A: Lymph nodes, spleen and mucosa-associated lymphoid tissues (MALT).", "front": "Examples of secondary lymphoid organs?"}, {"back": "A: Spleen.", "front": "Principal secondary lymphoid organ for filtration of blood-borne antigens and pathogens?"}, {"back": "A: Cortex, paracortex and medulla (enclosed within a fibrous capsule).", "front": "Three structural regions of a lymph node?"}, {"back": "A: Sites where mature lymphocytes encounter antigens, become activated and initiate immune responses.", "front": "What are secondary (peripheral) lymphoid organs?"}, {"back": "A: Apoptosis.", "front": "Fate of DP cells that fail positive selection?"}, {"back": "A: B cells organised into primary follicles.", "front": "What does the lymph node cortex contain?"}, {"back": "A: Lymph = Local (tissue infections); Spleen = Systemic (blood infections).", "front": "Mnemonic: Lymph = ? / Spleen = ?"}, {"back": "A: RBCs and platelets.", "front": "Blood components normally absent from lymph?"}, {"back": "A: Weeks 20–24 of gestation (replacing foetal liver).", "front": "When does bone marrow become the dominant site of haematopoiesis?"}, {"back": "A: CD4– CD8– double negative (DN) T-lineage cells.", "front": "ETPs differentiate into?"}, {"back": "A: Notch signals.", "front": "Signals ETPs receive from thymic epithelial cells?"}, {"back": "A: Thymus medullary region.", "front": "Thymic region where DP cells undergo negative selection?"}, {"back": "A: Bone marrow and thymus.", "front": "Primary lymphoid organs in humans?"}, {"back": "A: Rich in T cells; contains high endothelial venules (HEVs).", "front": "What characterises the lymph node paracortex?"}, {"back": "A: Tonsils and adenoids.", "front": "Examples of nasal-associated lymphoid tissue (NALT)?"}, {"back": "A: Training developing T cells to recognise non-self antigens while maintaining tolerance to self antigens.", "front": "What is “T-cell education”?"}, {"back": "A: Antigens (Ag)", "front": "Collective term for foreign substances that trigger immune defence?"}, {"back": "A: Filtering lymph and mounting immune responses against tissue-borne pathogens.", "front": "Function of lymph nodes (LNs)?"}, {"back": "A: Foetal liver.", "front": "Principal haematopoietic organ from around weeks 6–8 of gestation?"}, {"back": "A: Sternum, ribs, vertebrae, pelvis and proximal ends of long bones (femur and humerus).", "front": "Bones containing red bone marrow in adults?"}, {"back": "A: Allow selective migration of naïve B and T cells from the bloodstream into the lymph node parenchyma.", "front": "Role of addressins?"}, {"back": "A: CD4+ CD8+ double positive (DP) cells.", "front": "DN cells differentiate into?"}, {"back": "A: A single efferent lymphatic vessel at the hilum.", "front": "Exit route of lymph from a lymph node?"}, {"back": "A: Specialised post-capillary venules that express adhesion molecules (addressins) on their endothelial surface.", "front": "What are high endothelial venules (HEVs)?"}, {"back": "A: Fluid derived from interstitial fluid originating from blood plasma; contains immune cells and proteins.", "front": "What is lymph?"}, {"back": "A: Sites where lymphocytes are generated, develop and mature.", "front": "What are primary (central) lymphoid organs?"}, {"back": "A: Behind the sternum, between the lungs and anterior to the heart, within the anterior mediastinum.", "front": "Location of the thymus?"}, {"back": "A: B and T cells, plasma cells and macrophages arranged in medullary cords and sinuses.", "front": "Contents of the lymph node medulla?"}, {"back": "A: Active B-cell proliferati