ile limiting collateral damage to host tissues", "front": "How does the complement system balance pathogen elimination and host protection?"}, {"back": "They recognise acetylated human serum albumin, DNA, and damaged cells, form complexes with MASPs, and participate in opsonisation and inflammation", "front": "Why are ficolins crucial for first-line defence?"}, {"back": "As a rare, acquired, life-threatening blood disease characterised by complement-mediated destruction of red blood cells (haemolysis) when CD55 or CD59 is deficient or inhibited", "front": "How is paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria (PNH) characterised in the lecture?"}, {"back": "Factor D", "front": "Which enzyme cleaves Factor B in both the fluid-phase C3(H2O)Bb and surface-bound C3bBb convertases?"}, {"back": "Because complement activation is enzymatically explosive, so regulatory proteins limit convertase formation and stability to protect host tissues", "front": "Why is complement regulation necessary during activation?"}, {"back": "It continuously and spontaneously generates low-level C3(H2O) in plasma independently of antibodies to detect foreign surfaces upon contact", "front": "Why is the alternative pathway \\"tick-over\\" mechanism described as a surveillance system?"}, {"back": "They increase blood vessel permeability and stimulate histamine release from mast cells", "front": "How do C3a, C4a, and C5a contribute to inflammation at the tissue level?"}, {"back": "They insert into the lipid bilayer and polymerise to form a transmembrane pore (C5b-9)", "front": "How do C9 monomers form the MAC pore?"}, {"back": "Factor I", "front": "Which protease degrades C3b into inactive fragments with Factor H acting as a cofactor?"}, {"back": "A glycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI) anchor (it is a GPI-anchored membrane glycoprotein)", "front": "What structural feature anchors CD59 (protectin) to the cell membrane?"}, {"back": "Soluble (plasma) proteins and membrane-associated (membrane-bound) proteins", "