n Test, and urea & electrolyte", "front": "What blood and lab tests are listed under investigation of jaundice?"}, {"back": "Ultrasound if bile duct dilates >6 mm (obstruction); MRCP/EUS; liver biopsy if bile duct normal; CT/MRI if malignancy suspected", "front": "When is ultrasound or MRCP/endoscopic ultrasound or liver biopsy or CT/MRI used in jaundice investigation?"}, {"back": "Total bilirubin = Direct bilirubin + Indirect bilirubin", "front": "How is total bilirubin calculated from direct and indirect bilirubin?"}, {"back": "Obstruction of the biliary tree, Dubin-Johnson syndrome, Rotor syndrome, and Liver diseases such as various types of hepatitis", "front": "List causes of conjugated hyperbilirubinemia"}, {"back": "Abdominal pain, weight loss, fever, urine/stool color, itching, medications, IV drug use, alcohol, tattoos, sexual activity, blood transfusion, contact with jaundiced individual, family history of jaundice/anemia, previous surgery, and travel", "front": "What history points are part of evaluating a jaundiced patient?"}, {"back": "ALP increased later, GGT normal to decrease, urine/stool urobilinogen normal to increase", "front": "What are ALP, GGT, and urobilinogen in hepatocellular jaundice?"}, {"back": "Glucuronyl transferase absent, no bilirubinuria, rapid death (kernicterus), none or phenobarbitone or ultraviolet light or liver transplant", "front": "What are the liver defect, bilirubinuria, prognosis, and management of Crigler-Najjar Type I?"}, {"back": "Autosomal recessive, any age, mild conjugated hyperbilirubinemia", "front": "What are the inheritance, age of presentation, and hyperbilirubinemia type/severity of Dubin-Johnson syndrome?"}, {"back": "Autosomal recessive, neonate, very severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia", "front": "What are the inheritance, age of presentation, and hyperbilirubinemia type/severity of Crigler-Najjar Type I?"}, {"back": "Presence of bile pigments in urine; occurs in Hepatitis and obstruction of common bile duct", "front": "What is choluria and when does choluric jaundice occur?"}, {"back": "Water insoluble and does not pass into urine", "front": "What are the solubility and urine properties of unconjugated bilirubin bound to albumin?"}, {"back": "Increased unconjugated bilirubin; no bilirubin in urine", "front": "What are the bilirubin and urine bilirubin findings in hemolytic jaundice?"}, {"back": "Above 3 mg/dl (50 µmol/L)", "front": "At what serum bilirubin level is jaundice clinically manifested?"}, {"back": "Hemolytic disease of the newborn (hemolytic jaundice)", "front": "In the exercise (neonate of Rh-negative mother with immediate jaundice, pallor, low Hb, total bilirubin 15 mg/dl, indirect 12 mg/dl, direct 3 mg/dl, normal ALP, increased urobilinogen and fecal stercobilinogen), what is the likely diagnosis?"}, {"back": "250–350 mg/dl", "front": "How much unconjugated bilirubin is produced daily?"}, {"back": "Infective hepatitis (hepatitis A in this case) with hepatocellular jaundice and mild intrahepatic cholestasis", "front": "In Case 2 (21-year-old female returning from Asia with fever, vomiting, RUQ pain, tender hepatomegaly, dark urine with bilirubin and increased urobilinogen, ALT 560, bilirubin 110, ALP 190, GGT 60), what is the diagnosis?"}, {"back": "Passes through liver and is excreted in urine", "front": "What happens to the small amount of stercobilinogen absorbed from the bowel?"}, {"back": "AST+ALT normal, ALP normal, GGT normal, stool/urobilinogen increased, reticulocytosis, low HGB & haptoglobin, high LDH", "front": "What are AST, ALT, ALP, GGT, stool, and hemolysis markers in hemolytic jaundice?"}, {"back": "Pre-Hepatic, Hepatic, and Post hepatic", "front": "What are the three classifications of jaundice according to site of problem?"}, {"back": "Viral hepatitis, Drugs, Cirrhosis, and Tumors", "front": "What are major causes of hepatic jaundice?"}, {"back": "Less than 0.3 mg/dl", "front": "What is the normal direct bilirubin level?"}, {"back": "