ise cytokine networks and activity?"}, {"back": "CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes", "front": "Which T cells recognise MHC-I-peptide complexes on the cell surface?"}, {"back": "They remain CD3- and differentiate into other lymphocyte lineages such as B cells or NK cells", "front": "What happens to lymphoid precursors that do not follow the thymic TCR-CD3 pathway?"}, {"back": "Transporter associated with antigen processing (TAP)", "front": "Which transporter moves peptides from the cytosol into the ER lumen in the endogenous pathway?"}, {"back": "Antibodies, TCR, MHC molecules, CD3, CD4, CD8, Costimulatory Receptor (CD28), VCAM-1, and ICAMs", "front": "List examples of IgSF members from the source."}, {"back": "Activation of naïve CD8+ T cells by APCs that have presented exogenous antigens on MHC-I molecules", "front": "What is cross-priming?"}, {"back": "Both use V(D)J recombination and junctional diversity, but BCR also undergoes somatic hypermutation and class switching, which TCR lacks", "front": "How do diversity generation mechanisms compare between BCR and TCR?"}, {"back": "Phagoendolysosomes", "front": "What forms when phagosomes fuse with late endosomes and lysosomes?"}, {"back": "Selective uptake via receptors forming membrane-bound vesicles to transport materials inside", "front": "What is receptor-mediated endocytosis?"}, {"back": "TCR α and β chains confer antigen recognition, whereas CD3 γ and δ chains function solely in signal transduction", "front": "Why must TCR α and β chains not be confused with CD3 γ and δ chains?"}, {"back": "\\"Cell eating\\" of large particles via membrane-bound vesicles", "front": "What is phagocytosis?"}, {"back": "Assists in exchanging CLIP for antigenic peptides", "front": "What is the role of HLA-DM in the MHC-II pathway?"}, {"back": "TCRs recognise only processed peptide fragments presented by MHC molecules, whereas BCRs recognise native (unprocessed) antigens", "front": "How does TCR antigen recognition differ from BCR antige