d development", "front": "In which condition is thymus transplantation particularly beneficial and why?"}, {"back": "No; in both natural and artificial passive immunity, the recipient does not actively generate an immune response against the infectious agent", "front": "Does the recipient mount an active immune response to the infectious agent in passive immunity?"}, {"back": "Immunity where the body receives ready-made antibodies from another source instead of making them itself, acting instantly but fading after weeks or months because no memory cells are made", "front": "What is passive immunity?"}, {"back": "Natural (after recovering from an infection) and artificial (following vaccination)", "front": "What are the two types of active immunity listed in the source?"}, {"back": "Transfer of soluble immune factors, particularly antibodies, from mother to child, such as through colostrum (first milk) or across the placenta", "front": "What is natural passive immunity?"}, {"back": "Maternal antibodies steadily decline while the infant's own antibody production progressively increases, becoming more functional and capable of effective levels around 7–8 months of age", "front": "How do maternal antibody levels and infant antibody production change after birth?"}, {"back": "A medical procedure that removes leukocytes (WBC) or specific immune/stem cells from blood using an automated cell separator while returning red blood cells, plasma, and platelets to the body", "front": "What is leukapheresis?"}, {"back": "Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation (formerly called bone marrow transplantation; now also using peripheral blood and umbilical cord blood) to replace defective/absent HSCs with healthy donor stem cells to reconstitute haematopoietic and immune systems", "front": "What is HSCT and what are its stem cell sources mentioned in the source?"}, {"back": "Because a newborn's immune system is not fully developed at birth and matures gradually over time", "front": "Why is colostrum important for a newborn?"}, {"back": "\\"In a living thing\\"", "front": "What does \\"in vivo\\" mean in the final slide?"}, {"back": "Macrophages, granulocytes, T cells, and B cells, which may aid local immune defence and immunoregulation, especially in the gastrointestinal tract", "front": "Which immune cells are found in human milk and may transfer to the breast-fed infant?"}, {"back": "\\"Out of a living thing\\"", "front": "What does \\"ex vivo\\" mean in the final slide?"}, {"back": "\\"In glass\\"", "front": "What does \\"in vitro\\" mean in the final slide?"}, {"back": "Red blood cells, plasma, and platelets", "front": "Which blood components are returned to the body during leukapheresis?"}, {"back": "Transfer of primed or sensitised immune cells, particularly lymphocytes, from an immune individual to a recipient to confer antigen-specific immune function", "front": "What is adoptive immunity?"}, {"back": "Donor compatibility, timing of transplantation, risk of graft-versus-host disease (GvHD), conditioning regimen, and the patient's clinical condition", "front": "Which factors influence successful HSCT outcomes?"}, {"back": "An immunotherapeutic strategy where immune effector cells are collected, expanded and/or genetically modified ex vivo, then reinfused into the patient to enhance immune responses", "front": "What is adoptive cell therapy (ACT)?"}], "color": "blue", "folder": 0}], "folders": [{"icon": "stethoscope", "name": "Micro", "color": "blue", "parent": null}]}	2026-11-27 01:05:07.467113+00	folder	e30b86c4-7515-46e7-bcda-f3b139361e87	\N	1ee99020-5a2f-4701-bdf1-ec41b9d24eac
