ne response and mediated by phagocytic cells", "front": "How is antibody production initiated in response to T-dependent antigens?"}, {"back": "A fundamental mechanism transmitting extracellular signals from cytokines and growth factors directly to the nucleus to regulate gene expression", "front": "What is the JAK-STAT signalling pathway?"}, {"back": "Cross-linking of surface immunoglobulin (B-cell receptor, BCR) by foreign antigens", "front": "What is the first signal required for B-cell activation?"}, {"back": "Immune system regulation, cell growth and proliferation, cell differentiation, cell death (apoptosis), and tissue repair", "front": "Which cellular processes involve the JAK-STAT pathway according to the source?"}, {"back": "JAK activation, where receptor dimerisation brings associated JAKs together to autophosphorylate each other and phosphorylate the receptor", "front": "What is Step 2 of the JAK-STAT signalling cascade?"}, {"back": "Signal binding of a cytokine or ligand to its cell-surface receptor, inducing receptor dimerisation (or conformational rearrangement bringing subunits into close proximity)", "front": "What is Step 1 of the JAK-STAT signalling cascade?"}, {"back": "It drives resting B cells into the cell cycle, is essential for T-dependent antigen responses, and is required for immunoglobulin isotype (class) switching and memory B-cell generation", "front": "What are the roles of CD40–CD40L (CD154) interaction on B cells?"}, {"back": "STAT dimerisation, where phosphorylated STATs form homodimers (two identical STATs) or heterodimers (two different STATs)", "front": "What is Step 4 of the JAK-STAT signalling cascade?"}, {"back": "Transcription factors activated downstream of JAK signalling: STAT1, STAT2, STAT3, STAT4, STAT5A, STAT5B, and STAT6", "front": "What are STATs and what are the seven STAT proteins?"}, {"back": "It is crucial for innate and adaptive immune responses (inflammation, cell differentiation, pathogen defence), and dysregulation leads to immune disorders and autoimmunity", "front": "Why is the JAK-STAT pathway important in immunity and what happens when dysregulated?"}, {"back": "BCL2 and c-Myc", "front": "Which specific genes involved in cell division are mentioned in connection with high STAT3 activity?"}, {"back": "Eosinophils (via IgER and IL-5) and mast cells (via IgE) in parasitic infections and allergy", "front": "Which effector cells and conditions are linked to IgE and Th2 cytokines in the diagram?"}, {"back": "IgE, IgA, and IgG (with IL-5, IFN-γ, and TGF-β indicated near B-cell differentiation)", "front": "Which immunoglobulin classes are shown produced by B cells under Th2 influence in the diagram?"}, {"back": "Polycythemia vera (PV), essential thrombocythemia (ET), and myelofibrosis (MF)", "front": "Which three myeloproliferative neoplasms are specifically named under JAK-STAT dysregulation?"}, {"back": "IgM and IgG", "front": "Which immunoglobulin classes are shown produced by B cells under Th1 influence in the diagram?"}, {"back": "CD40, MHC-II, and CD58 on the APC interacting with CD40L, TCR/CD4, and CD2 on Th0", "front": "Which adhesion/co-stimulatory molecules appear on the APC and T cell in the Th0 interaction diagram?"}, {"back": "Homodimers consist of two identical STAT proteins, whereas heterodimers consist of two different STAT proteins", "front": "What is the difference between homodimers and heterodimers in STAT dimerisation?"}, {"back": "Non-specific phagocytosis engulfs antigen via pseudopodium, phagosome, lysosome, and phagolysosome formation, whereas specific phagocytosis involves antigen binding to surface immunoglobulin/receptor on the APC", "front": "What is the difference between non-specific phagocytosis and specific phagocytosis in the immune response diagram?"}, {"back": "IFN-γ (from Th1) inhibits Th2, and IL-10 (from Th2) inhibits Th1", "front": "How do Th1 and Th2 cytokines mutually inhibit each other in the diagram?"}, {"back": "They phosphorylate 