 phenol-chloroform after DNA release?"}, {"back": "At 4°C.", "front": "How is DNA stored for short-term stability?"}, {"back": "A260/A280 ratio.", "front": "Which ratio is used to assess DNA purity?"}], "color": "green", "folder": 1}, {"name": "Jaundice", "cards": [{"back": "Unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia and Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia", "front": "What are the two classifications of jaundice according to type of circulating bilirubin?"}, {"back": "Yellowish discoloration of sclera, skin and mucous membrane due to increased plasma bilirubin", "front": "What is jaundice?"}, {"back": "Ultrasound if bile duct dilates >6 mm (obstruction); MRCP/EUS; liver biopsy if bile duct normal; CT/MRI if malignancy suspected", "front": "When is ultrasound or MRCP/endoscopic ultrasound or liver biopsy or CT/MRI used in jaundice investigation?"}, {"back": "Metabolized by colonic bacteria to stercobilinogen, oxidized to stercobilin, and both are excreted in stool", "front": "How is conjugated bilirubin metabolized and excreted in stool?"}, {"back": "Bilirubin increased (50% conjugated), bilirubin in urine, AST+ALT+LDH usually modestly increased, ALP marked increase (↑↑↑ >3 upper limit), GGT markedly increased (↑↑↑↑↑↑), no urobilinogen", "front": "What are the bilirubin, enzyme, and urobilinogen findings in cholestatic jaundice?"}, {"back": "Abdominal pain, weight loss, fever, urine/stool color, itching, medications, IV drug use, alcohol, tattoos, sexual activity, blood transfusion, contact with jaundiced individual, family history of jaundice/anemia, previous surgery, and travel", "front": "What history points are part of evaluating a jaundiced patient?"}, {"back": "Hemolysis", "front": "What are major causes of prehepatic jaundice?"}, {"back": "Viral hepatitis, Drugs, Cirrhosis, and Tumors", "front": "What are major causes of hepatic jaundice?"}, {"back": "Autosomal recessive, any age, mild conjugated hyperbilirubinemia", "front": "What are the inheritance, age of presentation, and hyperbilirubinemia type/severity of Dubin-Johnson syndrome?"}, {"back": "CBC, blood film, reticulocyte count, clotting profile, Coombs test, Liver Function Test, and urea & electrolyte", "front": "What blood and lab tests are listed under investigation of jaundice?"}, {"back": "Increased conjugated + unconjugated bilirubin, AST+ALT marked increase (↑↑↑), bilirubin in urine", "front": "What are the bilirubin, transaminases, and urine bilirubin findings in hepatocellular jaundice?"}, {"back": "Infective hepatitis (hepatitis A in this case) with hepatocellular jaundice and mild intrahepatic cholestasis", "front": "In Case 2 (21-year-old female returning from Asia with fever, vomiting, RUQ pain, tender hepatomegaly, dark urine with bilirubin and increased urobilinogen, ALT 560, bilirubin 110, ALP 190, GGT 60), what is the diagnosis?"}, {"back": "ALP increased later, GGT normal to decrease, urine/stool urobilinogen normal to increase", "front": "What are ALP, GGT, and urobilinogen in hepatocellular jaundice?"}, {"back": "Magnetic Resonance CholangioPancreatography", "front": "What does MRCP stand for?"}, {"back": "Autosomal dominant, neonate, severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia", "front": "What are the inheritance, age of presentation, and hyperbilirubinemia type/severity of Crigler-Najjar Type II?"}, {"back": "Passes through liver and is excreted in urine", "front": "What happens to the small amount of stercobilinogen absorbed from the bowel?"}, {"back": "Physiological hyperbilirubinemia, Pathological hyperbilirubinemia, and Congenital hyperbilirubinemia", "front": "What are the three other classifications of jaundice mentioned?"}, {"back": "Glucuronyl transferase reduced (occasional mild hemolysis or defective bilirubin uptake), no bilirubinuria, normal life-span, no management needed (phenobarbitone occasionally)", "front": "What are the liver defect, bilirubinuria, prognosis, and management of Gilbert's syndrome?"}, {"back": "AST+ALT normal, ALP normal, GGT normal, stool/urobilinogen increased, reticulocytosis, low HGB & haptoglobin, high LDH", "front": "What are AST, ALT, ALP, GGT, stool, and hemolysis markers in hemolytic jaundice?"}, {"back": "Glucuronyl transferase greatly reduced, no bilirubinuria, can survive to adulthood, phenobarbitone/ultraviolet light/liver transplant", "front": "What are the liver defect, bilirubinuria, prognosis, and management of Crigler-Najjar Type II?"}, {"back": "Autosomal dominant, any age, mild conjugated hyperbilirubinemia", "front": "What are the inheritance, age of presentation, and hyperbilirubinemia type/severity of Rotor syndrome?"}, {"back": "Autosomal recessive, neonate, very severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia", "front": "What are the inheritance, age of presentation, and hyperbilirubinemia type/severity of Crigler-Najjar Type I?"}, {"back": "Jaundice, dark urine, and pale stool", "front": "What are the early clinical features of cholestasis?"}, {"back": "Fever, rigors, and pain", "front": "What are the clinical features of cholangitis and hepatic abscess in cholestasis?"}, {"back": "Presence of bile pigments in urine; occurs in Hepatitis and obstruction of common bile duct", "front": "What is choluria and when does choluric jaundice occur?"}, {"back": "Increased production, Impaired hepatic uptake/conjugation (toxin/virus), and Impaired excretion (intrahepatic/extrahepatic)", "front": "What are the three main cause categories of jaundice?"}, {"back": "Water insoluble and does not pass into urine", "front": "What are the solubility and urine properties of unconjugated bilirubin bound to albumin?"}, {"back": "Above 3 mg/dl (50 µmol/L)", "front": "At what serum bilirubin level is jaundice clinically manifested?"}, {"back": "Pre-Hepatic, Hepatic, and Post hepatic", "front": "What are the three classifications of jaundice according to site of problem?"}, {"back": "Only in the presence of an excess of unconjugated bilirubin", "front": "When does acholuric jaundice occur?"}, {"back": "Hemolytic anemias, Neonatal physiological jaundice, Crigler-Najjar syndromes types I and II, Gilbert syndrome, and Toxic hyperbilirubinemia", "front": "List causes of unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia"}, {"back": "Hemolytic disease of the newborn (hemolytic jaundice)", "front": "In the exercise (neonate of Rh-negative mother with immediate jaundice, pallor, low Hb, total bilirubin 15 mg/dl, indirect 12 mg/dl, direct 3 mg/dl, normal ALP, increased urobilinogen and fecal stercobilinogen), what is the likely diagnosis?"}, {"back": "Defective bilirubin uptake and reduced intrahepatic binding, bilirubinuria present, normal life-span, none", "front": "What are the liver defect, bilirubinuria, prognosis, and management of Rotor syndrome?"}, {"back": "Pruritus, xanthelasma and xanthomata, malabsorption, weight loss, steatorrhea, osteomalacia, and bleeding tendency", "front": "What are the late clinical features of cholestasis?"}, {"back": "Total bilirubin = Direct bilirubin + Indirect bilirubin", "front": "How is total bilirubin calculated from direct and indirect bilirubin?"}, {"back": "UDP-glucuronyl transferase into bilirubin mono and diglucuronide", "front": "Which enzyme conjugates bilirubin in the endoplasmic reticulum and into what forms?"}, {"back": "Autosomal dominant, young adult (any), mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia", "front": "What are the inheritance, age of presentation, and hyperbilirubinemia type/severity of Gilbert's syndrome?"}, {"back": "Hemolytic anemia, Hemolytic disease of newborn, and Ineffective erythropoiesis (pernicious anemia)", "front": "Give examples of increased bilirubin production causes of jaundice"}, {"back": "Signs of chronic disease, hepatic encephalopathy, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, ascites, and palpable gall bladder", "front": "What physical examination findings are checked in a jaundiced patient?"}, {"back": "Increased unconjugated bilirubin; no bilirubin in urine", "front": "What are the bilirubin and urine bilirubin findings in hemolytic jaundice?"}, {"back": "Obstruction of the biliary tree, Dubin-Johnson syndrome, Rotor syndrome, and Liver diseases such as various types of hepatitis", "front": "List causes of conjugated hyperbilirubinemia"}, {"back": "3–17 µmol/L (0.2–1 mg/dl)", "front": "What is the normal total serum bilirubin level?"}, {"back": "Gallstones, Cancer of bile ducts, Carcinoma of ampulla of Vater, and Carcinoma of head of pancreas", "front": "What are major causes of posthepatic jaundice?"}, {"back": "Glucuronyl transferase absent, no bilirubinuria, rapid death (kernicterus), none or phenobarbitone or ultraviolet light or liver transplant", "front": "What are the liver defect, bilirubinuria, prognosis, and management of Crigler-Najjar Type I?"}, {"back": "Reduced canalicular excretion of organic anions (e.g., bilirubin, cholecystographic contrast agents), bilirubinuria present, normal life-span, none", "front": "What are the liver defect, bilirubinuria, prognosis, and management of Dubin-Johnson syndrome?"}, {"back": "Heme", "front": "What molecule does bilirubin break down from?"}, {"back": "Gilbert syndrome; absent urinary bilirubin indicates unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, normal reticulocytes exclude hemolytic anemia, and raised ALP is of bone origin expected for age", "front": "In Case 1 (13-year-old boy with fever, myalgia, poor intake, jaundice, bilirubin 60 µmol/L, no urinary bilirubin, normal reticulocytes, ALP 180 U/L), what is the diagnosis and ALP explanation?"}, {"back": "250–350 mg/dl", "front": "How much unconjugated bilirubin is produced daily?"}, {"back": "Less than 0.3 mg/dl", "front": "What is the normal direct bilirubin level?"}], "color": "red", "folder": 1}], "folders": [{"icon": "flask-conical", "name": "Biochemistry 3", "color": "blue", "parent": null}, {"icon": "flask-conical", "name": "العملي", "color": "violet", "parent": 0}]}	2026-11-25 11:41:09.606198+00	folder	fb1154a4-42ab-461d-83bd-d7a1b473628e	\N	32df672c-36d2-4f4e-a5d1-8153ec68d3ad
8a062b2d	Micro	{"decks": [{"name": "intro", "cards": [{"back": "A: Yolk sac (weeks 3–5 of gestation).", "front": "First site of haematopoiesis in early embryogenesis?"}, {"back": "A: Afferent lymphatic vessels.", "front": "Vessels through which lymph enters a lymph node?"}, {"back": "A: Peyer’s patches and mesenteric lymph nodes.", "front": "Examples of gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT)?"}, {"back": "A: Lymph nodes, spleen and mucosa-associated lymphoid tissues (MALT).", "front": "Examples of secondary lymphoid organs?"}, {"back": "A: Spleen.", "front": "Principal secondary lymphoid organ for filtration of blood-borne antigens and pathogens?"}, {"back": "A: Cortex, paracortex and medulla (enclosed within a fibrous capsule).", "front": "Three structural regions of a lymph node?"}, {"back": "A: Sites where mature lymphocytes encounter antigens, become activated and initiate immune responses.", "front": "What are secondary (peripheral) lymphoid organs?"}, {"back": "A: Apoptosis.", "front": "Fate of DP cells that fail positive selection?"}, {"back": "A: B cells organised into primary follicles.", "front": "What does the lymph node cortex contain?"}, {"back": "A: Lymph = Local (tissue infections); Spleen = Systemic (blood infections).", "front": "Mnemonic: Lymph = ? / Spleen = ?"}, {"back": "A: RBCs and platelets.", "front": "Blood components normally absent from lymph?"}, {"back": "A: Weeks 20–24 of gestation (replacing foetal liver).", "front": "When does bone marrow become the dominant site of haematopoiesis?"}, {"back": "A: CD4– CD8– double negative (DN) T-lineage cells.", "front": "ETPs differentiate into?"}, {"back": "A: Notch signals.", "front": "Signals ETPs receive from thymic epithelial cells?"}, {"back": "A: Thymus medullary region.", "front": "Thymic region where DP cells undergo negative selection?"}, {"back": "A: Bone marrow and thymus.", "front": "Primary lymphoid organs in humans?"}, {"back": "A: Rich in T cells; contains high endothelial venules (HEVs).", "front": "What characterises the lymph node paracortex?"}, {"back": "A: Tonsils and adenoids.", "front": "Examples of nasal-associated lymphoid tissue (NALT)?"}, {"back": "A: Training developing T cells to recognise non-self antigens while maintaining tolerance to self antigens.", "front": "What is “T-cell education”?"}, {"back": "A: Antigens (Ag)", "front": "Collective term for foreign substances that trigger immune defence?"}, {"back": "A: Filtering lymph and mounting immune responses against tissue-borne pathogens.", "front": "Function of lymph nodes (LNs)?"}, {"back": "A: Foetal liver.", "front": "Principal haematopoietic organ from around weeks 6–8 of gestation?"}, {"back": "A: Sternum, ribs, vertebrae, pelvis and proximal ends of long bones (femur and humerus).", "front": "Bones containing red bone marrow in adults?"}, {"back": "A: Allow selective migration of naïve B and T cells from the bloodstream into the lymph node parenchyma.", "front": "Role of addressins?"}, {"back": "A: CD4+ CD8+ double positive (DP) cells.", "front": "DN cells differentiate into?"}, {"back": "A: A single efferent lymphatic vessel at the hilum.", "front": "Exit route of lymph from a lymph node?"}, {"back": "A: Specialised post-capillary venules that express adhesion molecules (addressins) on their endothelial surface.", "front": "What are high endothelial venules (HEVs)?"}, {"back": "A: Fluid derived from interstitial fluid originating from blood plasma; contains immune cells and proteins.", "front": "What is lymph?"}, {"back": "A: Sites where lymphocytes are generated, develop and mature.", "front": "What are primary (central) lymphoid organs?"}, {"back": "A: Behind the sternum, between the lungs and anterior to the heart, within the anterior mediastinum.", "front": "Location of the thymus?"}, {"back": "A: B and T cells, plasma cells and macrophages arranged in medullary cords and sinuses.", "front": "Contents of the lymph node medulla?"}, {"back": "A: Active B-cell proliferation, somatic hypermutation and class switching.", "front": "Processes occurring in germinal centres?"}, {"back": "A: CD4 or CD8 single positive (SP) T cells.", "front": "Form in which mature T cells exit the thymus?"}], "color": "blue", "folder": 0}, {"name": "Cells of the immune", "cards": [{"back": "A : Combat helminths, involved in allergic reactions, promote IgE production", "front": "Th2 cells?"}, {"back": "A : Thymic cortex", "front": "Where does positive selection of T cells occur?"}, {"back": "A : CD28 on the T cell and B7 on the APC", "front": "Molecules involved in co-stimulation?"}, {"back": "A : Thymic medulla; establishes central tolerance by eliminating strongly self-reactive T cells", "front": "Where does negative selection occur, and what does it establish?"}, {"back": "A : Fight intracellular pathogens by activating macrophages and inducing cell-mediated immunity", "front": "Th1 cells?"}, {"back": "A : Dendritic cells, macrophages and B cells", "front": "Professional APCs?"}, {"back": "A : CD40–CD40L signalling and cytokine secretion", "front": "Signalling between B cells and helper T cells in T-cell–dependent activation?"}, {"back": "A : Suppress immune responses to prevent autoimmunity and maintain tolerance", "front": "Treg cells?"}, {"back": "A : Chemotaxis → adherence and pseudopodium formation → engulfment and phagosome formation → phagosome–lysosome fusion → elimination or antigen processing", "front": "Steps of phagocytosis?"}, {"back": "A : Robust antibody responses, class switching, affinity maturation and memory B cells", "front": "Outcomes of T-cell–dependent activation?"}, {"back": "A : Fibroblasts and endothelial cells", "front": "Non-professional APCs?"}, {"back": "A : Apoptosis (death by neglect)", "front": "Fate of thymocytes that fail to recognise self-MHC?"}, {"back": "A : Becomes a CD4+ SP helper T cell (CD8 downregulated)", "front": "Fate of a DP thymocyte whose TCR recognises MHC-II?"}, {"back": "A : αβ TCR non-covalently associated with the CD3 signalling complex", "front": "Receptor complex on mature naïve CD4+ and CD8+ T cells?"}, {"back": "A : In germinal centres (spleen and LNs) during the primary immune response", "front": "Where are memory B cells generated?"}, {"back": "A : Toll-like receptor 9 (TLR9)", "front": "RBC surface receptor for nucleic acid sensing?"}, {"back": "A : Immune cell that captures, digests and displays foreign protein fragments on MHC molecules to activate T cells", "front": "Antigen-presenting cell (APC)?"}, {"back": "A : Perforin forms pores; granzymes enter the cytosol", "front": "Perforin vs granzymes?"}, {"back": "A : Medullary thymic epithelial cells and dendritic cells", "front": "Cells presenting self-peptides during negative selection?"}, {"back": "A : IgM (generally no immunological memory)", "front": "Antibody predominantly induced by T-cell–independent activation?"}, {"back": "A : T-cell dependent and T-cell independent", "front": "Two types of B-cell activation?"}, {"back": "A : Th1, Th2, Th17, Tfh and Treg", "front": "Principal CD4+ T helper subsets?"}, {"back": "A : Becomes a CD8+ SP cytotoxic T cell (CD4 downregulated)", "front": "Fate of a DP thymocyte whose TCR recognises MHC-I?"}, {"back": "A : DP thymocytes must recognise self-MHC with low to moderate affinity", "front": "Requirement for passing positive selection?"}, {"back": "A : Cross-presentation and enhanced APC activation, often dependent on CD4+ T-cell help", "front": "Antigens from non-replicating viruses require what to activate CD8+ T cells?"}, {"back": "A : Monocytes (macrophages, dendritic cells), neutrophils and eosinophils", "front": "Key phagocytic cells?"}, {"back": "A : They lack antigen-presenting capacity, cytokine synthesis and proliferative responses", "front": "Why are RBCs not classical immune cells?"}, {"back": "A : Complement interactions, immune complex transport, modulation of inflammatory mediators and nucleic acid sensing", "front": "How do RBCs influence immunity?"}, {"back": "A : Protein antigens", "front": "Antigens involved in T-cell–dependent activation?"}, {"back": "A : Perforin–granzyme pathway and Fas (CD95)–FasL (CD95L) interaction", "front": "Two mechanisms of CTL-mediated killing?"}, {"back": "A : B-cell activation and antibody production within germinal centres", "front": "Tfh cells?"}, {"back": "A : Antigen recognition, co-stimulation and cytokines", "front": "Three signals of T-cell activation?"}, {"back": "A : Non-protein antigens such as polysaccharides and lipopolysaccharides", "front": "Antigens involved in T-cell–independent activation?"}, {"back": "A : Protect against extracellular bacteria and fungi by recruiting neutrophils", "front": "Th17 cells?"}, {"back": "A : Persist long term and enable a faster, more robust secondary immune response", "front": "Role of memory B cells?"}, {"back": "Granulocyte-monocyte progenitors (GMPs) and megakaryocyte-erythroid progenitors (MEPs)", "front": "What are the two main progenitor types derived from common myeloid progenitors (CMPs)?"}, {"back": "CLPs form pro-B cells (Ig heavy chain D-J then V-DJ recombination), progress to pre-B cells (light chain recombination), become immature B cells expressing surface IgM (sIgM), exit the bone marrow as transitional B cells, and mature in peripheral lymphoid organs into naïve B cells expressing both sIgM and sIgD", "front": "What are the sequential stages of B-cell development from CLP to mature naïve B cell?"}, {"back": "Driven by gene rearrangements, notably V(D)J recombination of Ig genes; BCR is sIgM on immature B cells and both sIgM and sIgD on mature B cells", "front": "What drives B-cell maturation and which BCR isotypes are expressed on immature versus mature B cells?"}, {"back": "Monocyte-derived dendritic cells, conventional dendritic cells (cDCs), and plasmacytoid dendritic cells (pDCs), with cDCs and pDCs arising primarily from the common dendritic cell precursor (CDP)", "front": "Which dendritic cell subsets exist and where do they primarily arise from?"}, {"back": "T cells regulate virtually all aspects of the immune response with some subsets having cytotoxic (\\"killer\\") activity; B cells differentiate into plasma cells that produce antibodies", "front": "What are the main functional roles of T cells and B cells?"}, {"back": "They expand in the foetal liver and ultimately colonise the bone marrow to establish lifelong haematopoiesis", "front": "Where do definitive HSCs expand and establish lifelong haematopoiesis?"}, {"back": "Initially in the yolk sac, then in the foetal liver, and finally in the bone marrow", "front": "Where do common lymphoid progenitors (CLPs) derive from HSCs across development?"}, {"back": "By eliminating or inactivating B cells that bind strongly to self-antigens via apoptosis (clonal deletion), receptor editing (additional light-chain rearrangement), or anergy (functional unresponsiveness)", "front": "How does negative selection establish central tolerance in developing B cells?"}, {"back": "T cells mature in the thymus; B cells mature in the bone marrow in mammals (and in the bursa of Fabricius in birds)", "front": "Where do T cells and B cells mature and acquire immunological competence?"}, {"back": "Common myeloid progenitors (CMPs) and common lymphoid progenitors (CLPs)", "front": "What are the two main progenitor lineages that HSCs differentiate into?"}, {"back": "Myeloblasts (giving granulocytes: neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils) and monoblasts (giving monocytes, which form macrophages and dendritic cells)", "front": "Which cells arise from granulocyte-monocyte progenitors (GMPs)?"}, {"back": "They undergo T-cell receptor (TCR) gene rearrangement and express the CD3–TCR complex, making mature T cells CD3-positive (CD3+)", "front": "What happens to T-lineage cells in the thymus and how are mature T cells classified?"}, {"back": "Basal (\\"tonic\\") BCR signalling indicating a functional receptor capable of supporting survival, whereas inadequate signalling generally leads to apoptosis", "front": "What is required for positive selection of developing B cells?"}, {"back": "Neutrophils, monocytes, macrophages, and dendritic cells", "front": "Which cells are listed as phagocytes central to the innate immune response?"}, {"back": "Because they do not undergo thymic selection and never express the CD3–TCR complex", "front": "Why are natural killer (NK) cells not considered T cells despite sharing a common lymphoid origin?"}, {"back": "Like all nucleated cells, B cells express MHC-I to present endogenous antigens to CD8+ cytotoxic T cells, and as professional APCs they express MHC-II to present exogenous (internalised) antigens to CD4+ helper T cells", "front": "How can B cells act as antigen-presenting cells (APCs) via MHC-I and MHC-II?"}, {"back": "Multipotent stem cells that give rise to all blood cell lineages", "front": "What are haematopoietic stem cells (HSCs)?"}, {"back": "In the yolk sac", "front": "Where does primitive haematopoiesis first occur during embryonic development?"}, {"back": "Migration to the thymus and receipt of Notch-mediated signals", "front": "What signal and migration are required for CLPs to commit to the T-cell lineage?"}, {"back": "Early T-lineage progenitors (ETPs) commit via Notch signalling, progress through double-negative (DN; CD4– CD8–) stages with TCR β-chain rearrangement, become double-positive (DP; CD4+ CD8+) thymocytes with α-chain rearrangement, and undergo positive and negative selection to become single-positive (SP) CD4+ or CD8+ mature naïve T cells", "front": "What are the sequential developmental stages of T cells in the thymus from ETP to mature naïve T cells?"}, {"back": "They exit the bone marrow and migrate to secondary lymphoid organs (such as the spleen and lymph nodes) to become mature naïve B cells", "front": "Where do immature B cells that pass bone marrow checkpoints go to complete maturation?"}, {"back": "Transforming growth factor beta (TGF-β)", "front": "Which factor negatively regulates HSC proliferation?"}, {"back": "They remain CD3-negative (CD3–) and may differentiate into other lymphoid lineages, including B cells and natural killer (NK) cells", "front": "What happens to CLPs that do not enter the thymus?"}, {"back": "They develop from bone marrow HSCs via CLPs without antigen receptor gene rearrangement (CD3–), and mature NK cells acquire cytotoxic machinery (perforin, granzymes) and cytokine-producing capacity (IFN-γ) for rapid innate defence", "front": "How do NK cells develop and what effector functions do mature NK cells acquire?"}, {"back": "Erythrocytes (RBCs) and megakaryocytes (which give platelets)", "front": "Which cells arise from megakaryocyte-erythroid progenitors (MEPs)?"}, {"back": "In the aorta-gonad-mesonephros (AGM) region, an embryonic mesodermal site between the notochord and somites that develops into the dorsal aorta, genital ridges, and mesonephros", "front": "Where do definitive haematopoietic stem cells (HSCs) emerge in mammals, including humans?"}, {"back": "Stem cell factor (SCF), erythropoietin (EPO), thrombopoietin (TPO), FLT3 ligand (binding FLT3/CD135), GM-CSF, IL-3, and IL-7", "front": "Which hematopoietic cytokines regulate HSC maintenance, self-renewal, and differentiation?"}, {"back": "MHC-I × CD8 = 8 and MHC-II × CD4 = 8", "front": "What is the \\"Rule of 8\\" mnemonic in the lecture?"}], "color": "green", "folder": 0}, {"name": "Molecules of the immune", "cards": [{"back": "A : Type I (immediate) hypersensitivity, including anaphylaxis, and immunity against helminths", "front": "Main roles of IgE?"}, {"back": "A : Cleaves below the hinge → one bivalent F(ab’)2 fragment and small degraded Fc pieces", "front": "Pepsin digestion of IgG?"}, {"back": "A : Polymeric immunoglobulin receptor (pIgR)", "front": "Receptor that transports dimeric IgA across epithelial cells?"}, {"back": "A : An antigen that induces tolerance", "front": "Tolerogen?"}, {"back": "A : Heavy (H) and light (L) chains held together by disulfide bonds", "front": "Basic immunoglobulin structure?"}, {"back": "A : IgG", "front": "Only Ig that efficiently crosses the placenta?"}, {"back": "A : Complete antigens that both induce an immune response and react with its products", "front": "Immunogens?"}, {"back": "A : Toxic shock syndrome", "front": "Example of superantigen-induced immunopathology?"}, {"back": "A : IgG1 and IgG3", "front": "IgG subclasses that activate the classical complement pathway?"}, {"back": "A : The antigen-binding site of an antibody or BCR that interacts with the epitope", "front": "Paratope?"}, {"back": "A : Microbial proteins (e.g. bacterial exotoxins) that non-specifically activate large numbers of T cells, causing massive cytokine release", "front": "Superantigens?"}, {"back": "A : Agents (e.g. aluminium salts) that enhance immune responses by improving antigen presentation, activating innate pathways or creating antigen depots", "front": "Adjuvants?"}, {"back": "A : TCRs recognise peptide epitopes presented by MHC molecules", "front": "How do TCRs recognise antigens, unlike antibodies and BCRs?"}, {"back": "A : Protects IgA from proteolytic degradation in mucosal secretions", "front": "Function of the secretory component of IgA?"}, {"back": "A : Within the MHC", "front": "Where are most immune response (Ir) genes located?"}, {"back": "A : Substances recognised as foreign by the immune system that can elicit an immune response", "front": "Antigens?"}, {"back": "A : γ = IgG, α = IgA, μ = IgM, ε = IgE, δ = IgD", "front": "Heavy chains and their Ig classes (GAMED)?"}, {"back": "A : TCR Vβ region and MHC-II outside the peptide-binding cleft (no intracellular processing)", "front": "Where do superantigens bind?"}, {"back": "A : IgG (70–75%)", "front": "Most abundant Ig in adult serum?"}, {"back": "A : IgE (0.01%)", "front": "Least abundant Ig in serum?"}, {"back": "A : Dimer linked by a J chain", "front": "Form of IgA at mucosal surfaces?"}, {"