ing on exposure site and nature)—while IgM changes relatively little", "front": "Which antibodies predominate in the secondary response and how do IgM levels change?"}, {"back": "IFN-γ, TGF-β, and several interleukins, which influence the antibody class produced", "front": "What regulates Ig isotype (class) switching?"}, {"back": "Th1-derived IL-2 and Th2-derived cytokines such as IL-4, IL-5, IL-6, IL-10, and IL-13", "front": "Which cytokines drive B-cell proliferation and differentiation?"}, {"back": "It processes the bacterium via the exogenous pathway, loads peptide fragments onto MHC-II molecules, and transports peptide–MHC-II complexes to the APC surface for presentation to CD4+ helper T cells", "front": "How does an APC process a phagocytosed bacterium and present it to CD4+ helper T cells?"}, {"back": "Excessive pathway activation regulates cell-proliferation genes (e.g., BCL2 and c-Myc with high STAT3 activity); overactive STAT3 is linked to higher melanoma recurrence risk post-treatment, and high STAT5 activity is associated with higher mortality likelihood in prostate cancer", "front": "How is elevated STAT3 or STAT5 activity linked to cancer progression and prognosis?"}, {"back": "Complement fixation and phagocytosis, antigen processing and presentation and CD4+ Th-cell activation, B-cell activation, B-cell proliferation and differentiation (plasma and memory cells), and antibody production", "front": "What are the five steps of cellular interactions for antibody synthesis in the source?"}, {"back": "Myeloproliferative neoplasms (polycythemia vera [PV], essential thrombocythemia [ET], myelofibrosis [MF]), rheumatoid arthritis, atopic dermatitis, and multiple malignancies", "front": "Which diseases are associated with JAK-STAT dysregulation, mutations, or persistent activation?"}, {"back": "They undergo clonal proliferation and differentiate into plasma cells capable of Ig isotype switching", "front": "What happens to B cells after activation?"}, {"back": "In mammary glands, JAK-STAT promotes cell division and reduces apoptosis during pregnancy and puberty, so excessive activation can form cancer; JAK2 mutations can lead to leukaemia and lymphoma", "front": "How can altered JAK-STAT signalling contribute to breast cancer or haematologic malignancies?"}, {"back": "Interleukin-12 (IL-12), naturally produced by APCs such as dendritic cells and macrophages", "front": "Which cytokine guides naïve T cells (Th0) to become Th1 cells?"}, {"back": "APC cytokine signals favouring Th2 differentiation, with IL-4 from activated T cells playing a central role and APC-derived IL-10 potentially supporting a Th2-skewed response", "front": "Which cytokines favour Th2 differentiation according to the source?"}, {"back": "Non-receptor tyrosine kinases associated with cytokine receptors that mediate intracellular signalling: JAK1, JAK2, JAK3, and TYK2", "front": "What are JAKs and what are the four members of the JAK family?"}, {"back": "Co-stimulatory interactions (such as CD40 on B cells interacting with CD40L/CD154 on activated T helper cells) and cytokines (such as IL-4 produced by Th2 cells)", "front": "What constitutes the second signal for B-cell activation?"}, {"back": "Gene transcription, where STAT dimers translocate into the nucleus, bind specific DNA sequences, and regulate transcription of target genes", "front": "What is Step 5 of the JAK-STAT signalling cascade?"}, {"back": "It facilitates fixation of complement component C3b, which acts as an opsonin and enhances phagocytosis by antigen-presenting phagocytic cells (APPC) such as macrophages", "front": "How does endotoxin (lipopolysaccharide) in Gram-negative bacterial cell walls aid phagocytosis?"}, {"back": "STAT recruitment and phosphorylation, where phosphorylated receptor tyrosines act as docking sites for STATs, which are then phosphorylated by activated JAKs", "front": "What is Step 3 of the JAK-STAT signalling cascade?"}, {"back": "It is initiated by the innate immune response and mediated by phagocytic cells", "front": "How is antibody production initiated in response to T-dependent antigens?"}, {"back": "A fundamental mechanism transmitting extracellular signals from cytokines and growth factors directly to the nucleus to regulate gene expression", "front": "What is the JAK-STAT signalling pathway?"}, {"back": "Cross-linking of surface immunoglobulin (B-cell receptor, BCR) by foreign antigens", "front": "What is the first signal required for B-cell activation?"}, {"back": "Immune system regulation, cell growth and proliferation, cell differentiation, cell death (apoptosis), and tissue repair", "front": "Which cellular processes involve the JAK-STAT pathway according to the source?"}, {"back": "JAK activation, where receptor dimerisation brings associated JAKs together to autophosphorylate each other and phosphorylate the receptor", "front": "What is Step 2 of the JAK-STAT signalling cascade?"}, {"back": "Signal binding of a cytokine or ligand to its cell-surface receptor, inducing receptor dimerisation (or conformational rearrangement bringing subunits into close proximity)", "front": "What is Step 1 of the JAK-STAT signalling cascade?"}, {"back": "It drives resting B cells into the cell cycle, is essential for T-dependent antigen responses, and is required for immunoglobulin isotype (class) switching and memory B-cell generation", "front": "What are the roles of CD40–CD40L (CD154) interaction on B cells?"}, {"back": "STAT dimerisation, where phosphorylated STATs form homodimers (two identical STATs) or heterodimers (two different STATs)", "front": "What is Step 4 of the JAK-STAT signalling cascade?"}, {"back": "Transcription factors activated downstream of JAK signalling: STAT1, STAT2, STAT3, STAT4, STAT5A, STAT5B, and STAT6", "front": "What are STATs and what are the seven STAT proteins?"}, {"back": "It is crucial for innate and adaptive immune responses (inflammation, cell differentiation, pathogen defence), and dysregulation leads to immune disorders and autoimmunity", "front": "Why is the JAK-STAT pathway important in immunity and what happens when dysregulated?"}, {"back": "BCL2 and c-Myc", "front": "Which specific genes involved in cell division are mentioned in connection with high STAT3 activity?"}, {"back": "Eosinophils (via IgER and IL-5) and mast cells (via IgE) in parasitic infections and allergy", "front": "Which effector cells and conditions are linked to IgE and Th2 cytokines in the diagram?"}, {"back": "IgE, IgA, and IgG (with IL-5, IFN-γ, and TGF-β indicated near B-cell differentiation)", "front": "Which immunoglobulin classes are shown produced by B cells under Th2 influence in the diagram?"}, {"back": "Polycythemia vera (PV), essential thrombocythemia (ET), and myelofibrosis (MF)", "front": "Which three myeloproliferative neoplasms are specifically named under JAK-STAT dysregulation?"}, {"back": "IgM and IgG", "front": "Which immunoglobulin classes are shown produced by B cells under Th1 influence in the diagram?"}, {"back": "CD40, MHC-II, and CD58 on the APC interacting with CD40L, TCR/CD4, and CD2 on Th0", "front": "Which adhesion/co-stimulatory molecules appear on the APC and T cell in the Th0 interaction diagram?"}, {"back": "Homodimers consist of two identical STAT proteins, whereas heterodimers consist of two different STAT proteins", "front": "What is the difference between homodimers and heterodimers in STAT dimerisation?"}, {"back": "Non-specific phagocytosis engulfs antigen via pseudopodium, phagosome, lysosome, and phagolysosome formation, whereas specific phagocytosis involves antigen binding to surface immunoglobulin/receptor on the APC", "front": "What is the difference between non-specific phagocytosis and specific phagocytosis in the immune response diagram?"}, {"back": "IFN-γ (from Th1) inhibits Th2, and IL-10 (from Th2) inhibits Th1", "front": "How do Th1 and Th2 cytokines mutually inhibit each other in the diagram?"}, {"back": "They phosphorylate 