w does C3b mediate opsonisation?"}, {"back": "Nascent C3b binds microbial surfaces lacking regulators via its thioester bond, binds Factor B (cleaved by Factor D) to form C3bBb, and is stabilised by properdin (Factor P)", "front": "How is the surface-bound alternative C3 convertase formed and stabilised?"}, {"back": "It is a GPI-anchored membrane glycoprotein that binds C8 and C9 to prevent C9 polymerisation, MAC formation, and cell lysis", "front": "How does CD59 (protectin) protect host cells from MAC?"}, {"back": "MASP-1 and MASP-2 (analogous to C1r and C1s); upon binding, MASP-2 activates and cleaves C4 and C2 like C1s to form C4b2a", "front": "Which proteases associate with MBL and ficolins and what does MASP-2 do?"}, {"back": "Approximately 1% of circulating C3 per hour, generating C3(H2O), which mimics the structure and function of C3b", "front": "At what rate does C3 undergo spontaneous hydrolysis in circulation and what does C3(H2O) mimic?"}, {"back": "It is antibody-independent and initiated by pattern recognition molecules—primarily mannose-binding lectin (MBL) and ficolins—binding conserved microbial carbohydrates", "front": "How is the lectin pathway initiated and is it antibody-dependent?"}, {"back": "It promotes dissociation of both classical and alternative pathway convertases", "front": "What is the role of decay-accelerating factor (CD55)?"}, {"back": "It attracts neutrophils, monocytes, and other immune cells to the site of infection to enhance inflammation", "front": "What is the chemotactic role of C5a?"}, {"back": "More than 30 soluble and membrane-bound proteins, including C1-C9 and regulators such as Factor H and MBL", "front": "How many proteins are in the complement system?"}, {"back": "C3(H2O) binds Factor B, which is cleaved by Factor D to form C3(H2O)Bb, cleaving native C3 into C3a and C3b", "front": "How is the fluid-phase C3 convertase formed in the alternative pathway?"}, {"back": "A larger fragment (\\"b\\") binding target surfaces and a smaller soluble fragment (\\"a\\") often with pro-inflammatory activity", "front": "What are the two fragments typically produced by proteolytic cleavage of complement proteins?"}, {"back": "Opsonisation, cell lysis, inflammation, chemotaxis, clearance of immune complexes and dead cells, and enhancement of adaptive immunity", "front": "What are the six main functions of the complement system?"}, {"back": "Classical, lectin, and alternative pathways, which converge on common enzymatic steps, notably C3 and C5 cleavage by convertases", "front": "What are the three complement activation pathways and where do they converge?"}, {"back": "Primarily mannose, N-acetyl-D-glucosamine, fucose, and glucose on pathogens, plus dying host cells and altered-self Ags", "front": "Which carbohydrates does MBL bind and what else does it recognise?"}, {"back": "C1q plus two molecules each of the serine proteases C1r and C1s", "front": "What is the composition of the calcium-dependent C1 complex?"}, {"back": "C5b sequentially binds C6 and C7 to form membrane-inserting C5b-7, then binds C8 for partial membrane penetration and C9 polymerisation into a transmembrane pore (C5b-9)", "front": "How does MAC assemble starting from C5b?"}, {"back": "It induces autoactivation of C1r, which cleaves and activates C1s, initiating the cascade by cleaving C4 and C2", "front": "What happens when C1q binds antibody Fc regions?"}, {"back": "A complex network of plasma and membrane-associated proteins forming a major effector arm of innate immunity and an essential amplifier of adaptive immune responses", "front": "What is the complement system (CS)?"}, {"back": "It acts as both an independent activation route and a powerful amplification loop for C3b generated by classical and lectin pathways", "front": "What dual role does the alternative pathway play?"}, {"back": "Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria (PNH), a rare, acquired, life-threatening blood disorder marked by complement-mediated red blood cell destruction (haemolysis)", "front": "What disease can result from deficiency or inhibition of CD55 or CD59?"}, {"back": "Ion imbalance, osmotic swelling, leakage of contents, and lysis of susceptible cells", "front": "What consequences result from MAC pore formation in a target cell membrane?"}, {"back": "It irreversibly inactivates C1r, C1s, and MASPs", "front": "How does C1 inhibitor (C1-INH) regulate complement?"}, {"back": "Additional C3b joins existing C3 convertases to form C4b2a3b (classical/lectin) or C3bBb3b (alternative), cleaving C5 into C5a and C5b", "front": "How are C5 convertases formed in the complement pathways?"}, {"back": "By forming the membrane attack complex (MAC), creating pores in bacterial membranes—especially Gram-negative bacteria—causing them to burst", "front": "How does the complement system cause cell lysis?"}, {"back": "It helps remove antigen-antibody complexes and damaged or apoptotic cells to prevent tissue accumulation", "front": "How does complement contribute to clearance of immune complexes and dead cells?"}, {"back": "It competes with Factor B for C3b binding and acts as a cofactor for Factor I-mediated cleavage of C3b into inactive fragments", "front": "How does Factor H regulate C3b?"}, {"back": "As inactive precursors (zymogens) sequentially activated by proteolytic cleavage", "front": "How do most complement proteins circulate in plasma before activation?"}, {"back": "It is a short-lived serine protease complex that catalyses cleavage of hundreds to thousands of C3 molecules", "front": "Why is C3 convertase (C4b2a) a major amplification step?"}, {"back": "C3a, C4a, and C5a, which increase blood vessel permeability and stimulate histamine release from mast cells (with C5a also a powerful inflammatory mediator)", "front": "Which complement fragments act as anaphylatoxins and what do they do?"}, {"back": "Through a sequential, enzyme-driven cascade that recognises danger, amplifies the response, and eliminates pathogens while limiting collateral damage to host tissues", "front": "How does the complement system balance pathogen elimination and host protection?"}, {"back": "They recognise acetylated human serum albumin, DNA, and damaged cells, form complexes with MASPs, and participate in opsonisation and inflammation", "front": "Why are ficolins crucial for first-line defence?"}, {"back": "As a rare, acquired, life-threatening blood disease characterised by complement-mediated destruction of red blood cells (haemolysis) when CD55 or CD59 is deficient or inhibited", "front": "How is paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria (PNH) characterised in the lecture?"}, {"back": "Factor D", "front": "Which enzyme cleaves Factor B in both the fluid-phase C3(H2O)Bb and surface-bound C3bBb convertases?"}, {"back": "Because complement activation is enzymatically explosive, so regulatory proteins limit convertase formation and stability to protect host tissues", "front": "Why is complement regulation necessary during activation?"}, {"back": "It continuously and spontaneously generates low-level C3(H2O) in plasma independently of antibodies to detect foreign surfaces upon contact", "front": "Why is the alternative pathway \\"tick-over\\" mechanism described as a surveillance system?"}, {"back": "They increase blood vessel permeability and stimulate histamine release from mast cells", "front": "How do C3a, C4a, and C5a contribute to inflammation at the tissue level?"}, {"back": "They insert into the lipid bilayer and polymerise to form a transmembrane pore (C5b-9)", "front": "How do C9 monomers form the MAC pore?"}, {"back": "Factor I", "front": "Which protease degrades C3b into inactive fragments with Factor H acting as a cofactor?"}, {"back": "A glycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI) anchor (it is a GPI-anchored membrane glycoprotein)", "front": "What structural feature anchors CD59 (protectin) to the cell membrane?"}, {"back": "Soluble (plasma) proteins and membrane-associated (membrane-bound) proteins", "