ne response and mediated by phagocytic cells", "front": "How is antibody production initiated in response to T-dependent antigens?"}, {"back": "A fundamental mechanism transmitting extracellular signals from cytokines and growth factors directly to the nucleus to regulate gene expression", "front": "What is the JAK-STAT signalling pathway?"}, {"back": "Cross-linking of surface immunoglobulin (B-cell receptor, BCR) by foreign antigens", "front": "What is the first signal required for B-cell activation?"}, {"back": "Immune system regulation, cell growth and proliferation, cell differentiation, cell death (apoptosis), and tissue repair", "front": "Which cellular processes involve the JAK-STAT pathway according to the source?"}, {"back": "JAK activation, where receptor dimerisation brings associated JAKs together to autophosphorylate each other and phosphorylate the receptor", "front": "What is Step 2 of the JAK-STAT signalling cascade?"}, {"back": "Signal binding of a cytokine or ligand to its cell-surface receptor, inducing receptor dimerisation (or conformational rearrangement bringing subunits into close proximity)", "front": "What is Step 1 of the JAK-STAT signalling cascade?"}, {"back": "It drives resting B cells into the cell cycle, is essential for T-dependent antigen responses, and is required for immunoglobulin isotype (class) switching and memory B-cell generation", "front": "What are the roles of CD40–CD40L (CD154) interaction on B cells?"}, {"back": "STAT dimerisation, where phosphorylated STATs form homodimers (two identical STATs) or heterodimers (two different STATs)", "front": "What is Step 4 of the JAK-STAT signalling cascade?"}, {"back": "Transcription factors activated downstream of JAK signalling: STAT1, STAT2, STAT3, STAT4, STAT5A, STAT5B, and STAT6", "front": "What are STATs and what are the seven STAT proteins?"}, {"back": "It is crucial for innate and adaptive immune responses (inflammation, cell differentiation, pathogen defence), and dysregulation leads to immune disorders and autoimmunity", "front": "Why is the JAK-STAT pathway important in immunity and what happens when dysregulated?"}, {"back": "BCL2 and c-Myc", "front": "Which specific genes involved in cell division are mentioned in connection with high STAT3 activity?"}, {"back": "Eosinophils (via IgER and IL-5) and mast cells (via IgE) in parasitic infections and allergy", "front": "Which effector cells and conditions are linked to IgE and Th2 cytokines in the diagram?"}, {"back": "IgE, IgA, and IgG (with IL-5, IFN-γ, and TGF-β indicated near B-cell differentiation)", "front": "Which immunoglobulin classes are shown produced by B cells under Th2 influence in the diagram?"}, {"back": "Polycythemia vera (PV), essential thrombocythemia (ET), and myelofibrosis (MF)", "front": "Which three myeloproliferative neoplasms are specifically named under JAK-STAT dysregulation?"}, {"back": "IgM and IgG", "front": "Which immunoglobulin classes are shown produced by B cells under Th1 influence in the diagram?"}, {"back": "CD40, MHC-II, and CD58 on the APC interacting with CD40L, TCR/CD4, and CD2 on Th0", "front": "Which adhesion/co-stimulatory molecules appear on the APC and T cell in the Th0 interaction diagram?"}, {"back": "Homodimers consist of two identical STAT proteins, whereas heterodimers consist of two different STAT proteins", "front": "What is the difference between homodimers and heterodimers in STAT dimerisation?"}, {"back": "Non-specific phagocytosis engulfs antigen via pseudopodium, phagosome, lysosome, and phagolysosome formation, whereas specific phagocytosis involves antigen binding to surface immunoglobulin/receptor on the APC", "front": "What is the difference between non-specific phagocytosis and specific phagocytosis in the immune response diagram?"}, {"back": "IFN-γ (from Th1) inhibits Th2, and IL-10 (from Th2) inhibits Th1", "front": "How do Th1 and Th2 cytokines mutually inhibit each other in the diagram?"}, {"back": "They phosphorylate each other and phosphorylate the cytokine receptor itself, creating phosphorylated tyrosine docking sites for STATs", "front": "What happens when JAK proteins autophosphorylate upon receptor dimerisation?"}, {"back": "Melanoma recurrence risk (overactive STAT3), prostate cancer mortality likelihood (high STAT5 activity), breast cancer (mammary JAK-STAT/STAT3), and leukaemia and lymphoma (JAK2 mutations)", "front": "Which specific cancers are linked to elevated STAT3, elevated STAT5, or JAK2 mutations in the lecture?"}, {"back": "IL-4 acts in both autocrine and paracrine manners on Th2, alongside IL-3, IL-5, and IL-6", "front": "Which cytokines act in an autocrine and paracrine manner on Th2 cells in the pathway diagram?"}, {"back": "IL-2 acts in both autocrine and paracrine manners on Th1, alongside IFN-γ, GM-CSF, and TNF", "front": "Which cytokines act in an autocrine and paracrine manner on Th1 cells in the pathway diagram?"}], "color": "red", "folder": 0}, {"name": "Passive and active immunity", "cards": [{"back": "\\"In a computer\\"", "front": "What does \\"in silico\\" mean in the final slide?"}, {"back": "Because the host's own immune system is fully responsible for recognising the antigen and actively mounting an effective immune response", "front": "Why is active immunity termed \\"active\\"?"}, {"back": "Before birth, primarily IgG crosses the placenta; after birth, IgA is transferred through breast milk, especially IgA-rich colostrum in the first few days after delivery", "front": "How are maternal immunoglobulins transferred to the infant across two main stages?"}, {"back": "Diphtheria antitoxin, mAbs protecting against respiratory syncytial virus (RSV), and antivenom given after snake bites", "front": "What examples of artificial passive immunisation are given in the source?"}, {"back": "Transfer of antibodies, such as immune serum or monoclonal antibodies (mAbs), from an immune individual or manufactured source to a non-immune (naïve) recipient", "front": "What is artificial passive immunity?"}, {"back": "Because the transferred antibodies are gradually degraded and no immunological memory is established", "front": "Why is protection from artificial passive immunisation temporary?"}, {"back": "Immunity where the host's immune system makes its own antibodies after encountering a germ, taking weeks to start but creating memory cells for long-term or lifelong protection", "front": "What is active immunity?"}, {"back": "Natural transfer of immune cells, artificial transfer of immune cells, and cellular transplantation for immune reconstitution", "front": "What are the three types of adoptive transfer of immunity listed?"}, {"back": "No; the full extent and functional significance of this cellular transfer in healthy infants remain incompletely defined", "front": "Is the full functional significance of maternal milk cellular transfer settled in healthy infants?"}, {"back": "Thymus transplantation and haematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HSCT)", "front": "Which two cellular transplantations are mentioned for immune reconstitution in severe immunodeficiency?"}, {"back": "It is an established treatment for certain haematological malignancies and under active investigation for solid tumours and other diseases", "front": "What is the clinical status of adoptive cell therapy according to the source?"}, {"back": "Through low stomach acidity, physical buffering, and rapid gastric emptying, with roughly 27% to 30% surviving gastric transit to reach the infant's intestines alive", "front": "How do maternal milk cells survive passage through the baby's stomach and what proportion survives?"}, {"back": "Activation of lymphocytes, production of specific antibodies, and formation of immunological memory leading to usually long-term protection", "front": "Which processes are involved in active immunity?"}, {"back": "In selected patients with thymic aplasia, as the graft provides a microenvironment for T-lymphocyte maturation and development", "front": "In which condition is thymus transplantation particularly beneficial and why?"}, {"back": "No; in both natural and artificial passive immunity, the recipient does not actively generate an immune response against the infectious agent", "front": "Does the recipient mount an active immune response to the infectious agent in passive immunity?"}, {"back": "Immunity where the body receives ready-made antibodies from another source instead of making them itself, acting instantly but fading after weeks or months because no memory cells are made", "front": "What is passive immunity?"}, {"back": "Natural (after recovering from an infection) and artificial (following vaccination)", "front": "What are the two types of active immunity listed in the source?"}, {"back": "Transfer of soluble immune factors, particularly antibodies, from mother to child, such as through colostrum (first milk) or across the placenta", "front": "What is natural passive immunity?"}, {"back": "Maternal antibodies steadily decline while the infant's own antibody production progressively increases, becoming more functional and capable of effective levels around 7–8 months of age", "front": "How do maternal antibody levels and infant antibody production change after birth?"}, {"back": "A medical procedure that removes leukocytes (WBC) or specific immune/stem cells from blood using an automated cell separator while returning red blood cells, plasma, and platelets to the body", "front": "What is leukapheresis?"}, {"back": "Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation (formerly called bone marrow transplantation; now also using peripheral blood and umbilical cord blood) to replace defective/absent HSCs with healthy donor stem cells to reconstitute haematopoietic and immune systems", "front": "What is HSCT and what are its stem cell sources mentioned in the source?"}, {"back": "Because a newborn's immune system is not fully developed at birth and matures gradually over time", "front": "Why is colostrum important for a newborn?"}, {"back": "\\"In a living thing\\"", "front": "What does \\"in vivo\\" mean in the final slide?"}, {"back": "Macrophages, granulocytes, T cells, and B cells, which may aid local immune defence and immunoregulation, especially in the gastrointestinal tract", "front": "Which immune cells are found in human milk and may transfer to the breast-fed infant?"}, {"back": "\\"Out of a living thing\\"", "front": "What does \\"ex vivo\\" mean in the final slide?"}, {"back": "\\"In glass\\"", "front": "What does \\"in vitro\\" mean in the final slide?"}, {"back": "Red blood cells, plasma, and platelets", "front": "Which blood components are returned to the body during leukapheresis?"}, {"back": "Transfer of primed or sensitised immune cells, particularly lymphocytes, from an immune individual to a recipient to confer antigen-specific immune function", "front": "What is adoptive immunity?"}, {"back": "Donor compatibility, timing of transplantation, risk of graft-versus-host disease (GvHD), conditioning regimen, and the patient's clinical condition", "front": "Which factors influence successful HSCT outcomes?"}, {"back": "An immunotherapeutic strategy where immune effector cells are collected, expanded and/or genetically modified ex vivo, then reinfused into the patient to enhance immune responses", "front": "What is adoptive cell therapy (ACT)?"}], "color": "blue", "folder": 0}], "folders": [{"icon": "stethoscope", "name": "Micro", "color": "blue", "parent": null}]}	2026-11-27 01:05:07.590657+00	folder	e30b86c4-7515-46e7-bcda-f3b139361e87	\N	1ee99020-5a2f-4701-bdf1-ec41b9d24eac
